WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.410
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:10.988
Good afternoon.

00:00:10.988 --> 00:00:11.780
I'm Phillip Khoury.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.630
I'm Associate Provost with
responsibility for the arts

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:15.540
here at MIT.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.250
And I want to welcome you
to the public presentation

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:25.400
by Olafur Eliasson of
the 2014 Eugene McDermott

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.820
Award in the Arts at MIT.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.130
We look forward to a
stimulating presentation

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.370
about the wide range of the
remarkable artist's work.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:38.210
The title of his presentation is
intriguing, to say the least--

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.530
Holding Hands with the Sun.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.140
Now before introducing
Olafur, let

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.220
me extend a special greeting
to Mrs. Margaret McDermott.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.240
She's a dear friend--

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.156
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:03.330
She is a dear friend,
extraordinary benefactor.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.680
A very special guest
who traveled all the way

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.440
from her home in Dallas
to be with us on this,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.980
the 40th anniversary of
the Eugene McDermott Award.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.580
I had the enormous privilege
of visiting with Margaret

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.120
in Dallas, just a few weeks ago
where she is the city's leading

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.800
patron of the arts.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.760
Her hospitality, incidentally,
is second to none.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.770
Accompanying Margaret
on this visit

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.720
is her daughter, Mary
Anne McDermott Cook,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.340
who is with us right here.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.660
Delighted you're here, Mary.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.850
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.740
Among her many activities
in Dallas and beyond,

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.270
she is president of the
Eugene McDermott Foundation.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.490
We're thrilled, really,
to have you, both of you,

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.470
returned here back to MIT.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:53.230
You honor us by being here.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.740
Let me say a few words
about the McDermott Award

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.410
and what the McDermott
family has done for MIT.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.350
Established in 1974
by Margaret McDermott

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.480
to honor her late
husband Eugene,

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.630
co-founder of Texas
Instruments, the McDermott Award

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.990
recognizes individuals whose
artistic trajectory and body

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.470
of work indicate that they
will achieve the highest

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.360
distinction in their fields.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.050
The award is considered an
investment in the recipient's

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.360
future creative
work, rather than

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.830
a prize for a particular project
or a lifetime of achievement.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.160
A special feature of the
award is the campus residency,

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.940
and an important focus, one
that Mrs. McDermott herself

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.170
has encouraged.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.080
That is the direct
interaction with students,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.660
a goal that has been
realized in exemplary ways

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.340
in this residency.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.910
Most of all, the McDermott
Award reflects MIT's commitment

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.900
to risk-taking, problem-solving,
and connecting creative minds

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.670
across disciplines.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.430
I just want to add that the
McDermott family have been

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.780
major contributors to MIT's top
ranked public art collection,

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.090
and the McDermott
Scholarship Endowment Fund

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.970
has supported more
MIT undergraduates

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:06.570
than any such fund at MIT.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:11.430
It really is a tribute to this
family and their support to us.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.090
Now permit me to recount
just a few aspects

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.940
of Olafur Eliasson's work,
some of which, of course,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.830
you just witnessed.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.220
I believe you'll understand why
he's such an ideal recipient

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.050
of the McDermott Award.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.050
His extraordinary body of
work encompasses a broad range

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.590
of creative practices spanning
art, science, and psychology.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.770
He has made strikingly original
contributions to public art,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:38.580
to architecture, and recently
to social entrepreneurship.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.320
His installation,
The Weather Project,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.080
which we just saw a bit of,
was visited by more than two

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.460
million people.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.430
For this project, he installed
an immense artificial sun

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.800
in London's Tate
Modern, transforming

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.270
that cavernous turbine hall
with a theory of light and fog,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:58.410
and producing a completely
unique, immersive environment.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:01.680
Through such phenomenological
investigations of light, color,

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.740
atmosphere and water, he has
probed the nature of space

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.080
and of human perception.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.210
Olafur Eliasson is
probably best known

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.280
in this country for
two exhibitions.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.760
One is Take Your
Time, Olafur Eliasson.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.910
It was a survey exhibition
organized in 2007

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.339
by the San Francisco
Museum of Art, which

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:23.940
traveled until 2010,
stopping in New York at MoMA

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.730
and at the Dallas Museum of Art.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.280
The other is New
York City Waterfalls,

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.560
commissioned by the
Public Art Fund in 2008

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.230
with the support of
then-Mayor Michael Bloomberg.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.750
It's a series of four
man-made waterfalls

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.690
in the East River in New York.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.150
Most recently, our
McDermott honoree

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.310
collaborated with
engineer Frederick Ottesen

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:47.550
who is with us this evening,
to develop Little Sun, which

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:48.810
you saw in the video.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:53.700
A portable solar powered LED
lamp for the 1.6 billion people

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.130
worldwide without
access to electricity,

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.540
which he calls a work of
art that works in life.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:05.400
You will understand why Olafur
Eliasson's work resonates

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.490
with the creative values
of the MIT community.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:12.570
We particularly admire the wide
ranging and ambitious nature

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.310
of his creative
practice, which spans

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:18.990
multiple domains without regard
for disciplinary boundaries.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.300
We admire the collaborative
enterprise that

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.610
drives his creative output.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.190
We admire his relentless
experimentation

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.770
with light, space,
movement, and the nature

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.110
of human perception,
which compels

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.900
audiences to engage
with their environment

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.730
and with one another.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:39.810
And we admire his confidence
that art makes a difference

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.570
in this world of ours.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:48.510
We look forward now to hearing
from Olafur Eliasson, our 2014

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.010
McDermott Award recipient.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.510
Olafur, please come
up, and a round

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:53.907
of applause for his
remarkable arts.

00:05:53.907 --> 00:05:57.386
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.290
Thank you so much.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:12.190
It's a long intro,
hard to follow.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.182
A lot has been said already.

00:06:14.182 --> 00:06:15.640
Thank you, Margaret,
for having me.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:16.690
You look so great today.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.040
It's very exciting to be here.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:23.050
I have, in fact,
been here already

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:25.810
for what's now the second day.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:31.300
And this is my fourth lecture,
and so I'm sort of warmed up.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:35.170
And it's been a big
experience to enjoy

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:37.770
the hospitality of MIT.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:41.040
Sort of hopping through the
departments and seeing all the

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.790
incredible, the massive
research going on,

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:48.360
and the degree of criticality
and self criticality.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.580
So that, of course,
is very inspiring.

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:59.140
Yeah, I've had a very, now I
have so much stuff in my head,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.960
so I hope I can, to
some extent, at least,

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.510
talk a little bit
about the works

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:05.770
that I brought on my computer.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:09.640
Because I have a lot
of new ideas now.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:14.890
But let me start by talking a
little bit about the project

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:18.400
that was briefly mentioned.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.790
It is 10 years old,
as of this winter.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:25.240
And it was a project
which, to me, meant

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.370
a sort of a strong
increase in the confidence

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:30.970
of the potential of what
creativity can actually do.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.710
And it also brought
me to this idea

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:36.340
that, well, maybe it's
to some extent about art,

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.680
but maybe it's
really about people.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.460
And I'm confident that
the systems in which

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.800
artwork, the museums
and galleries

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.190
and so on and so forth.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:49.420
But also the wider systems,
the general confidence

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:51.670
in creativity in
art and culture,

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:53.260
in the greater society.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.600
I'm sure that art has
something to offer which

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:58.870
is a kind of a model of
how we respect each other,

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:02.890
how we share things, how
we discuss with each other.

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:06.550
Art has something there, which
I think is highly valuable.

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:09.220
So I've become increasingly
involved with working

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.550
against the marginalization
of art and culture

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:15.220
in political systems,
in the sort of society

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:18.640
systems, the idea of what
is civic society, how

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.385
it is structured and so on.

00:08:20.385 --> 00:08:22.510
The reason why this project
here at the Tate Modern

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:26.260
was important for me was
that it for me showed

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:29.800
a space in which people came
in, the turbine hall in London,

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:33.460
it's an old power station,
the south side of the Thames.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:38.140
And the project opened,
and it was first

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:41.799
shown at the weather
forecast in the news.

00:08:41.799 --> 00:08:43.174
And as we know,
Brits in England,

00:08:43.174 --> 00:08:45.174
everybody loves the-- oh,
well like here today--

00:08:45.174 --> 00:08:46.600
everybody loves the weather.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.780
And the weather is interesting
because besides being

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:54.700
the nature, or the bit of
nature we have in cities--

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.060
you know, so it rains,
this is like the nature

00:08:58.060 --> 00:08:59.590
entering culture, if you want--

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:02.590
but the weather
is sort of a space

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:06.040
where we can actually have
a different experience

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:06.863
of the weather.

00:09:06.863 --> 00:09:09.280
Oh, I really hate when it
rains, and the person next to me

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:10.990
says, oh, I really
love when it rains,

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:12.580
and I still like
that other person.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:15.230
I say, oh, we can be together.

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:19.180
So interestingly, a
museum, or a platform

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:23.540
on which one can have
art, is, to some extent,

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.540
a success with regards
to facilitating

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:29.780
this ability to disagreeing
and sharing at the same time.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.540
So you can be singular--

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:33.700
this is my view on the world.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.550
And you can at the
same time be plural.

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:38.770
This is, I need to
share the world,

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:41.140
disregarding that I disagree.

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:43.460
And as we look at the
greater society, we of course

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:48.370
can see a lot of, how shall I
say, polarization, exclusion.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.693
Or if you disagree,
you're not welcome.

00:09:50.693 --> 00:09:52.360
If you're different,
you're not welcome.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.960
If you're not kind of
synchronizing with me,

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.070
I'm actually not going
to work with you.

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:01.360
So in this case, and I think
there is something about art

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.970
here, it was a space where
there was a highly singular,

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:06.220
or highly personal experience.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:07.750
People, they looked,
and they sort of

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:09.340
started behaving
differently than they

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:10.330
do in museums normally.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.163
As you can see, they
laid down on the floor.

00:10:12.163 --> 00:10:13.240
They stayed longer.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.870
They kind of brought in their
picnics, picnics on the floor.

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:19.160
And they sort of spent
time and used the museum

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:20.790
in different ways.

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:22.930
I would say it also
became a public space.

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:25.780
It was almost like the city
took a part of the museum

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:26.860
to its heart.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:28.630
The idea of public
being something

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:31.150
where you can have
both shared experiences

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:35.520
and individual experiences
became possible.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:38.280
I like this idea of
being singular and plural

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:39.095
at the same time.

00:10:39.095 --> 00:10:41.220
I think there's something
very Democratic about it.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.310
Something about sharing
values and also respecting

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:47.700
the individual needs.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.290
See, this is a small film.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:52.330
Let me see if I can turn it on.

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:54.580
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:58.510
So people are, as you can
tell, laying on the floor.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:03.570
The distance to the ceiling
is roughly 30 meters.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.300
Of course, looking up to the
ceiling 30 meters is something,

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.820
but you're sort of looking
down again with 30 meters down

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:09.320
again.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:12.900
So that means you're looking
60 meters and finding yourself

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:15.720
at a distance of 60
meters is exciting,

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.180
because you cannot help
seeing yourself in the context

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.200
of the space.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.990
So you're not just
looking for yourself.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:24.630
You are, in fact, also
seeing yourself in the,

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:28.680
let's say, in the greater
system of this space.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.670
It's hard to focus and talk and
listen to me at the same time.

00:11:38.738 --> 00:11:40.280
I could say a lot
about this project,

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:43.010
because also, I think
it's important to see

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:47.490
that looking at a work
of art almost as a,

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:51.217
I wouldn't say a machine,
but as a phenomena creator--

00:11:51.217 --> 00:11:51.800
[END PLAYBACK]

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:55.520
--in which the performance
of the audience

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:57.950
of the user, or the
spectator, becomes

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.980
the vehicle for that work is
an interesting idea, I think.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:05.210
I worked always, like, let's
see if we can hand over

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:08.360
a great degree of the
responsibility of making

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.210
the artwork to the people
who actually use it.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:15.950
Use is sort of pragmatic and,
it's largely demystifying.

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:18.380
But use in the sense
that you make an effort,

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:21.260
you come into the
museum, and you engage.

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:22.460
You might immerse yourself.

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.610
You engage in terms of,
you go look for something.

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:29.100
When you look at
a work of art, you

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.830
start a journey in which
your effort is requested.

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:32.970
So it's not like consuming.

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:34.980
It's not like sitting in
a Hollywood sort of movie

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.200
and just kind of all taking it
all in, doing nothing, right?

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:38.450
You have to work a little bit.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.890
You have to kind of give
a little bit of yourself.

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:44.070
If the artwork is
successful, I think

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:48.180
that it actually
succeeds in, how

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.930
should I say, verbalizing
something, or bringing language

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:54.390
to something which
you already feel.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.940
You kind of have it in
yourself, but you haven't quite

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:58.368
verbalized it
yourself yet, right?

00:12:58.368 --> 00:12:59.910
So you go and you
look for something,

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:03.097
and what you're looking
at is actually yourself.

00:13:03.097 --> 00:13:05.680
And of course this can then be
debated and so on and so forth.

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:08.010
But generally speaking,
a great work of art

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.050
somehow brings to
language something

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:12.210
that you already know,
but you haven't quite

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.088
figured out how to say it.

00:13:14.088 --> 00:13:15.130
So it's very interesting.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:17.870
It's almost like
it reflects you.

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:20.370
So in that sense, it's a little
bit, I wouldn't say therapy,

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:21.390
but I just don't--

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:24.190
it's a little bit, go
and you make an effort.

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:26.970
And sometimes the effort
to understand something

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:29.490
is more important than
the understanding itself.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.860
The effort, or the process,
or the time it takes for you

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.350
to kind of get involved--
and this is why this is such

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:35.520
an exciting little film--

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.710
the time it takes for
you to get involved

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:39.870
is as valuable as
the time for you

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:43.620
to kind of say, OK, now I
got it and I can go home.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:47.760
See, there is not so many spaces
in the world that facilitates

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:50.460
that type of activity.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.760
We have academia, we have great
academic sort of methodology.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.740
And we have places
like MIT where

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:59.490
people exercise the
questions and the critique

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:02.550
of the questions as an integral
part of the question, right?

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:04.057
And, of course,
structural thinking,

00:14:04.057 --> 00:14:05.640
structuralism and
postmodern thinking,

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:08.340
brought a lot of new tools
to the toolbox in which I

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:10.710
was educated-- the
times, the early '90s

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.750
and so on and so forth.

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:14.370
This is one of my
very first pieces.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:17.280
It's a rainbow at-- in
the lack of a better

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:21.480
photo-- it's just drizzling
water and a little spotlight.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:24.510
And as we know, a rainbow
takes a little more

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:27.150
than the spotlight and
the drizzling water takes.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:29.490
It also needs the eye.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.800
So you have the eye, this one.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:34.960
It's funny, the eye actually,
it's like a little machine.

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:35.460
Right?

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:38.130
You can even see the eye--
do you know this experiment?

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:40.710
Probably somebody from MIT
came up with that experiment.

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:42.418
If you press the
eyeball, you can sort of

00:14:42.418 --> 00:14:45.300
see how the image-- so there's
a little sort of spherical thing

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:46.680
in your head.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:49.710
Anyway, the eye, drop, light.

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:51.310
That makes a rainbow.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:53.220
So if I stand here,
I see one rainbow.

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:57.090
But my friend over here,
right, sees another rainbow.

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:59.460
So we can kind of agree,
OK, this is a rainbow.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:00.840
But it's not the same rainbow.

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:02.460
It's two different rainbows.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:05.760
And this is-- so,
so, so, so removing

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:10.090
the traditional object,
this is '93, making space

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:12.370
for the subject, or the
spectator, in this case.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:15.310
Suggesting that the producing
the eye component is

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:18.520
a part of the artwork
was almost suggesting,

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:22.120
well, maybe the artist,
maybe the biographical sort

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:23.830
of material, is less important.

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:26.500
Maybe the biographical
material of the spectator,

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:30.370
the working spectator,
the producing spectator,

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:34.030
the co-author is
the protagonist.

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:37.780
This became the beginning of
investigating public space,

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.280
different spaces, architecture.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:40.360
And this is a project.

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:41.260
This is in Sweden.

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:43.108
This is the
parliament in Sweden.

00:15:43.108 --> 00:15:44.650
The parliament sort
of on the island.

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:45.640
This is called Strom.

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:47.350
It's a lovely, very
clean water, Strom.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:49.990
The Swedes, they love their
clean water coming straight

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:51.850
up going into the ocean.

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:55.960
With a little bit of
non-toxic dye normally used

00:15:55.960 --> 00:16:01.480
for tracking ocean currents, you
could, sort of with an instant,

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:04.720
you can kind of dye
a whole river green.

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:07.300
And a number of artists has
been played with these things.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:12.390
And what I think is interesting
here is that this city as such

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:15.720
is, of course, when we think
about it, something organic.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:19.920
It's the life between
buildings that makes up a city.

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:21.790
What sometimes happened
is that the city

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:25.020
is so self obsessed
with its own image

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:28.230
that it kind of leans towards
something more static.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:29.820
We see a lot of
cities in Europe that

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:33.210
are, to some extent, at
least, turning into museums.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:36.150
This is a debate within
urbanistic planning,

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:38.970
and to what extent
those cities actually

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:42.390
represent the values of the
societies in which they are.

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:46.380
Or are they becoming, so let's
say, monuments of certain times

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:47.680
where other values was guiding?

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:50.700
So the idea of sort
of recalibrating

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:53.970
the depth of the space with
regards to its hospitality.

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:56.520
I thought I could just
toss a little bit of color

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:58.230
into a river like
that, and kind of with,

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:02.340
almost like a shock effect,
suggest, well, this space

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:03.420
is, in fact, organic.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:06.720
It moves, it passes by,
time is very important.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:09.569
If you have time, you
also have activity.

00:17:09.569 --> 00:17:12.460
If you have activity, there's
more space for people.

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:17.099
So in the great tradition of
very typically Scandinavian

00:17:17.099 --> 00:17:21.010
investigative methodology of
how to generate public space.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:24.660
This was, I think, a project
that I did again and again,

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:26.790
and with some success.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:28.930
Next day, the police in
Sweden wrote in the paper

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.930
that a lot of people have
called into the police

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:32.377
and asked why on
earth, et cetera.

00:17:32.377 --> 00:17:33.960
And the police have
calmed down people

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:36.990
saying that the
parliament actually had

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.755
a leak in the heating system.

00:17:38.755 --> 00:17:41.430
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:47.420
This is the Swedish way
of keeping people calm.

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:49.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:54.420
But so the idea of making things
explicit at the Green River.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:58.200
Making it explicit is
also having confidence

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:00.570
in the physicality of it.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:04.470
Instead of looking at a picture,
actually engaging in the,

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:06.000
let's say, the
water and the things

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:07.930
that are hard to understand.

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:08.880
So it's just time.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:09.800
Passing of time.

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:11.890
It's like, a little
bit abstract.

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:15.810
One of the great questions
today is like, everybody

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:18.270
knows what a glacier is.

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:20.850
Of course, some people even
have been on a glacier.

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:23.660
But everybody also knows, so
we have the climate crisis,

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:25.410
and we have the general
question about how

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:30.330
to understand what ice is,
and what the climate is,

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:32.560
and what the
ephemeral sort of is.

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:34.770
The changing of climate.

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:38.460
I'm a part of a generation
where when I was a child,

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:41.400
the climate was natural.

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:43.470
So it was considered,
OK, here I'm in culture,

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:45.070
and over there is nature.

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:46.020
This is interesting.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.670
And I was, you know,
when I grow up,

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:49.860
I hiked a lot with my father.

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.690
And so I went a lot to
a glaciers and so on.

00:18:52.690 --> 00:18:55.230
But as I grew older,
I realized, well,

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:56.500
the climate is not natural.

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:58.260
The climate is,
in fact, cultural.

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:02.925
It is, to some extent, an
increasingly accepted man-made.

00:19:02.925 --> 00:19:05.080
Or woman-made.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:08.640
And the thing here is,
I think the thing is,

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:10.980
the challenge lies
with, how do we make

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:14.280
things explicit from
theoretical knowledge

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:15.390
to physical knowledge?

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:17.940
Because that's an
old story within art.

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:21.480
And if you have an
idea here, oh, I

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:22.840
have an idea for a work of art.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:25.620
It's still not a work of
art until I've done it.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:28.000
The journey between thinking
and doing, as we know,

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:29.160
can be very wide.

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:30.660
And there's a lot
of steps in there

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:34.440
where you are sort of working
as a mechanical engineer,

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:34.980
if you want.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:35.910
The same thing, right?

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:37.020
Thinking, doing.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:39.160
And the steps, I think,
are very important.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:42.090
And when I've been here at
MIT, sort of on a side note,

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:44.850
it is all about bringing
thinking and doing together.

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:46.290
Bridging that gap.

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.630
So in this case, I thought,
well, people in Berlin

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:50.880
here can't go to Iceland.

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:53.760
So I'll just bring
the glacier to Berlin.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:56.370
So I froze down a
space, minus 10 degrees.

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:58.980
And then the glacial
blocks of the Vatnajokull,

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.147
they drops off the glacier as
it sort of falls into the ocean.

00:20:02.147 --> 00:20:04.230
And I could pick it up
with a tractor on the beach

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:07.410
and just ship it in a frozen
fish container to Berlin.

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:08.310
Very easy, actually.

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:12.660
And then I showed them, as you
can tell, it's so beautiful,

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:13.800
the ice itself.

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:15.750
And what is maybe
more important is

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:17.130
that you can actually touch it.

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:18.070
And it's incredible.

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:20.070
And you touch it, you
go, oh, it's really cold.

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:20.820
It's so funny.

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:22.290
And there is
something here which

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:26.220
is very sensitive, because
it's about bringing-- oh, we

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:27.390
knew it was cold.

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:28.080
No surprise.

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:30.540
But yet, when you touch
it, it is different

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:32.307
than from what you knew.

00:20:32.307 --> 00:20:33.390
So this is very important.

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:38.070
The kind of idea of bringing
knowledge and action together,

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:40.060
simply by touching.

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:42.570
So the title here, holding
hands with his son,

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:43.988
you can see where this is going.

00:20:43.988 --> 00:20:45.030
So let me just show this.

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:49.420
Now I have to [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:52.110
So this is, it's going
to be a little loud.

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:53.917
Or maybe not.

00:20:53.917 --> 00:20:55.750
Let me just say something
before I start it,

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:59.200
because I could tell before
you didn't listen when I was

00:20:59.200 --> 00:20:59.990
[INAUDIBLE].

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:01.810
So basically, ice floor.

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:04.270
Very big ice floor,
totally known.

00:21:04.270 --> 00:21:05.470
This is in Sao Paolo.

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:08.920
It's at the Sao Paolo
biennial quite a while ago.

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:12.130
So, interestingly, everybody,
like everybody is, as I said,

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:13.240
know about ice.

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:14.470
That's very easy.

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:15.760
From a distance, already.

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:16.877
Oh, it's ice.

00:21:16.877 --> 00:21:18.460
And then you sort
of, oh, interesting.

00:21:18.460 --> 00:21:20.200
Then you start running.

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:21.970
So very disruptive
to the exhibition.

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:25.960
Again, so people running
through the exhibition.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:27.790
And they're kind of
being loud, and so on.

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:29.860
And have a look at this.

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:31.737
And just see how I get it going.

00:21:31.737 --> 00:21:32.404
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:21:32.404 --> 00:21:34.774
- [WHOOPING, CHEERING, LAUGHING]

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:40.960
So it's very destabilizing.

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:44.530
And sorting out
your motoric skills,

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:47.338
disregarding that you know
very well what ice is.

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:57.815
So this is the
opening in Sao Paolo.

00:21:57.815 --> 00:22:01.210
And I was sort of wondering
what was going to happen.

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:06.060
And people are reconsidering
the rules [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:07.660
- [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:14.624
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:18.940
It was just too much.

00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:23.952
It sort of didn't fit the kind
of behavior construct of an art

00:22:23.952 --> 00:22:26.081
institution.

00:22:26.081 --> 00:22:27.440
I don't know if you can see.

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:28.815
The ice continues
on the outside.

00:22:28.815 --> 00:22:30.482
You can see in the
back of [INAUDIBLE],,

00:22:30.482 --> 00:22:31.745
there's a bit of ice outside.

00:22:31.745 --> 00:22:33.203
If you couldn't
pay, or didn't want

00:22:33.203 --> 00:22:35.712
to pay [INAUDIBLE] to
go in, you could also

00:22:35.712 --> 00:22:37.170
have a little skate
on the outside.

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:39.272
And that glass boundary
was interesting,

00:22:39.272 --> 00:22:42.008
because we know that glass is
a big social boundary and so

00:22:42.008 --> 00:22:42.920
on and so forth.

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:46.350
This is a Fantastic
Niemeyer building.

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:49.000
So this idea of
social boundaries.

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:59.270
So I don't mean
to, by all means,

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:01.370
there's not so much
ice in Sao Paolo.

00:23:01.370 --> 00:23:04.020
And I don't mean, you know,
there's so much other stuff,

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:05.270
so it's not really about that.

00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:10.220
But listen, the thing is the
discrepancy between knowledge

00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:12.200
and physical knowledge
became very clear here.

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.450
Because some people actually
sat down on the ice said,

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:16.940
oh, it's nice and cool
here in this hot space.

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:20.390
And the nice thing was they
said, oh, my god, the ice

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:22.110
is melting when you sit on it.

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:24.020
But so they knew perfectly
well what it was.

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:28.470
[INAUDIBLE] being.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:29.610
This is Sylvie Fleury.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:32.240
Do you know her?

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:36.530
So my home, my private
home video here.

00:23:36.530 --> 00:23:39.100
This is the curator being
a little bit concerned.

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:42.500
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:45.150
That was Adriano Pedrosa.

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:46.590
But let me see if I can--

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:48.000
[END PLAYBACK]

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:51.120
So these sorts of interventions
investigating the boundaries

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:55.860
of where do our, how far does
our tolerance, our hospitality,

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:58.110
our sense of space
go with regards to,

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:02.730
can we actually exercise the
idea of diversity we have?

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:06.120
Because we have such great
thoughts about how resourceful

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:08.350
we are with regards
to hospitality.

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:10.440
So we think, of course,
you could do anything

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:11.760
if you want in this museum.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.220
We can have any type
of exhibition you want,

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:17.790
as long as it's not noisy, of
course, and so on and so forth.

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:20.070
So, again, even our
values, the values

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:23.650
we carry with us through
society, sometimes we think,

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:26.400
oh if somebody falls
over on a bike, of course

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:29.047
I would go over and
help the person get up.

00:24:29.047 --> 00:24:31.255
But then you're on the street
and somebody falls over

00:24:31.255 --> 00:24:33.540
on a bike and we go, oh, my
god, this person is drunk.

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:36.060
Or can I help, or am I
going to get into-- so all

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:38.760
these sort of
boundaries between what

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:41.215
and how do you act
in public space.

00:24:41.215 --> 00:24:41.715
Right?

00:24:41.715 --> 00:24:43.030
It's very complex.

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:44.850
And of course, we would
want a public space

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:48.420
that actually facilitates
the trust in this way.

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:50.640
But the truth is, that's
not always the case.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:52.770
What type of space, what
type of architecture,

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:55.470
what type of pavement
actually facilitates

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:57.750
the values that we
think we have, but we

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:00.420
don't know actually how
to bring into doing?

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:02.560
This is a project,
this is Johannesberg.

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:03.870
It's also in the '90s.

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:05.910
I emptied out a small
rainwater reservoir

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:08.448
to kind of create the little
river that I called Erosion.

00:25:08.448 --> 00:25:09.240
Wasn't an accident.

00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:11.820
I was invited for a biennial,
had a nice little photo

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:13.410
piece about Scandinavia.

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:16.380
And I was walking around in
downtown derelict Johannesburg

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:18.030
at the time, '96,
I think it was.

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:19.880
Okwui Enwezor was the curator.

00:25:19.880 --> 00:25:21.630
And I said, oh my god,
this is so typical.

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:25.290
Here I am, all the way
from Berlin at the time,

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:26.100
in Johannesburg.

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:28.183
My god, I have to do
something different than just

00:25:28.183 --> 00:25:29.130
showing a photo.

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:32.702
Obviously a photo of Iceland,
you know, by all means.

00:25:32.702 --> 00:25:35.160
So I said, oh, look at this
incredible rainwater reservoir.

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:36.900
Why don't I rent a
little diesel pump

00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:40.050
and empty it out on the street
and see if I can make a river?

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:42.660
And of course, the
curator, I didn't ask him

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:45.180
because, of course,
because it was sort of,

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:46.410
it was interesting times.

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:49.980
So '96 Johannesburg, downtown
derelict squatter buildings.

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:53.160
You know, the Anglo American--
no, the Anglo-Saxon bank,

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:56.080
the Diamond Stock Exchange,
the diamond building.

00:25:56.080 --> 00:26:00.720
And very complex,
very complex area.

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:04.020
And I thought the idea
of erosion, time, river--

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:04.920
like a poem.

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:07.260
Like a little river that
just comes and goes,

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:08.070
was very beautiful.

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:10.470
It's rainwater, so
it wasn't really,

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:12.420
it rains enough for
collecting the water

00:26:12.420 --> 00:26:16.290
and using it for
public irrigation.

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:17.858
And this is the river.

00:26:17.858 --> 00:26:18.900
I can talk more about it.

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:21.210
As you can tell, it ran
roughly a mile and a half

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:23.640
through sort of organized
and unorganized areas,

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:26.280
and it ended up in the sewer.

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:35.840
This is in Los Angeles.

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:43.030
Lots of sun, morning, sort
of midday, late afternoon.

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:45.860
And if you walk in,
you can actually

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:47.390
see that the light is moving.

00:26:47.390 --> 00:26:49.500
It's very beautiful,
very good for LA.

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:50.870
You sort of stand there.

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:52.890
You go like, mm-hmm.

00:26:52.890 --> 00:26:53.900
Is this art?

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:56.890
And then you go,
and then you go--

00:26:56.890 --> 00:26:58.170
then it's like interesting.

00:26:58.170 --> 00:26:59.087
There's nothing there.

00:26:59.087 --> 00:27:02.120
And suddenly, your own presence
becomes quite explicit.

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.760
And you're so-- so,
so it's interesting.

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:07.560
Before, the light was over
there, and now it's here.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:10.260
Suddenly there's that
kind of cosmic connection.

00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:12.500
I won't overdo that,
but it's interesting,

00:27:12.500 --> 00:27:14.330
because it actually
does change a lot.

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:16.100
Have you been on
the street watching

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:19.520
how the pull of the
shadow of a streetlamp,

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:21.350
it actually moves,
like, very fast.

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:25.310
And that is the speed with
which the planet travels

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:27.200
through space around the sun.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:30.410
Obviously, as we
have come to learn,

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:31.920
the sun actually doesn't move.

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:33.450
It's the Earth that goes around.

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:37.940
So what you can see is,
in fact, you're traveling.

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:39.240
And it's time travel.

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:41.723
So to some extent,
it's the time it takes.

00:27:41.723 --> 00:27:43.140
So there's a lot
of stuff in here.

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:47.240
And I think what
we are, I guess,

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:50.030
to some extent with
regards to being abstract,

00:27:50.030 --> 00:27:54.870
making things explicit
also has to do with,

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:58.440
do we actually succeed
to see the planet?

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:01.350
Or sort of the idea of
the planet as one thing?

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:05.200
How do we identify with
the planet as one object?

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:07.530
So I've been very interested
in these sort of questions

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:10.320
from cosmology or
deep space cosmology.

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:13.990
How do you understand the planet
in the context of the universe?

00:28:13.990 --> 00:28:16.530
Now this gets a little
abstract, but the point here

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:18.347
is I think one of the
greater challenges

00:28:18.347 --> 00:28:20.430
with regards to, for
instance, the climate crisis,

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:21.330
we spoke about that.

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:22.980
The climate crisis
is that it does

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:25.890
require that you kind of think
of the planet as an object.

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:28.020
Sort of does, you do
need to sort of think

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:31.230
of everything at the same
time, which is very difficult.

00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:33.420
Because it's so difficult
enough to even understand

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:35.610
America has one
sort of ecosystem,

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:36.970
to see the whole thing.

00:28:36.970 --> 00:28:39.150
And this idea of
interdependence,

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:41.500
collectivity, the global
sort of idea of sharing,

00:28:41.500 --> 00:28:45.870
of sharing responsibility,
how does responsibility feel?

00:28:45.870 --> 00:28:48.300
It's very difficult. So
in that, and of course,

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:50.100
that's a far stretch
to say a spot of light

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:51.840
on the floor and
then responsibility.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:55.560
It's a bit-- but still, I
think, if one can actually

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.710
think of yourself in the
context of something planetary,

00:28:58.710 --> 00:29:02.760
if you want, think of Kepler.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:04.710
The work he did.

00:29:04.710 --> 00:29:07.950
And that was so
groundbreaking in a way.

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:10.200
Not that this is quite
on the scale of Kepler.

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:13.720
But I would like to I would
like to toss this work of art

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:16.530
into kind of trajectory
or the orbit of Kepler,

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:20.010
Kepler-ish kind of thinking.

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:20.910
Same question.

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:24.690
Glacier, glacier melting, river
running, aerial photography.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:28.110
And somewhere in the middle,
roughly here three rows up,

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:32.910
there's a dam being
built. This is a water--

00:29:32.910 --> 00:29:36.240
what you can-- hydroelectric
power dam being

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:37.680
built in Iceland.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:39.060
So this means the lower--

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:40.440
no, the upper half--

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:44.430
I mean, everything on the
left side and up from that dam

00:29:44.430 --> 00:29:46.770
is now underwater.

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:51.870
It's almost a mile deep on
the deepest part, 800 meters.

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:53.070
That's not quite a mile.

00:29:53.070 --> 00:29:54.720
But it's a very
deep at the deep.

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:57.730
And then everything else on
the right side is still there.

00:29:57.730 --> 00:30:02.550
So this is a little documentary
of a space that was,

00:30:02.550 --> 00:30:06.150
which is now underwater.

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:08.370
And of course, the whole
question of dam [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:08.370 --> 00:30:12.510
electricity, the company from
America built who's running

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:14.370
this to melt aluminum,
there's a very--

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:15.270
lot of debates.

00:30:15.270 --> 00:30:16.590
It's [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:19.740
So it's a complex photo
documenting something.

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:25.170
A black river on a
snowy white landscape.

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:28.380
So almost like a Japanese
calligraphic poem.

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:29.340
Chinese, if you want.

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:35.930
So, I have a handful
of works like this.

00:30:35.930 --> 00:30:38.110
I thought I will just
walk you through them.

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:39.970
There's not a lot left.

00:30:39.970 --> 00:30:43.240
And then we have roughly half
an hour for some questions.

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:43.740
You see?

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:44.260
Do you see?

00:30:44.260 --> 00:30:45.850
I mean, I always
wonder whether I

00:30:45.850 --> 00:30:48.940
should talk long for
a few works, or less

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:49.700
for a lot of work.

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:50.950
So now I'm kind of in between.

00:30:50.950 --> 00:30:51.970
I hope it's OK.

00:30:51.970 --> 00:30:55.090
I hope it's OK for you.

00:30:55.090 --> 00:30:56.770
This is the waterfalls
project that I

00:30:56.770 --> 00:31:00.730
was lucky to do in New York
together with the mayor's

00:31:00.730 --> 00:31:01.720
office.

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:05.110
A lot of people came together
and supported this project.

00:31:05.110 --> 00:31:07.700
One of the main supporters,
Aggie, who is here tonight.

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:09.470
It's great to see you, Aggie.

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:13.990
The waterfalls, I
thought, for me, it

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:16.630
was about claiming space.

00:31:16.630 --> 00:31:19.160
Taking space, simply.

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:21.790
I know from Iceland, when
I'm in front of a landscape,

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:23.747
and I look at the
landscape, and I always

00:31:23.747 --> 00:31:25.330
get so excited because
there's nothing

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:27.070
in the landscape that
kind of indicates

00:31:27.070 --> 00:31:28.660
the size of the
scale, the depth.

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:30.430
And you look at it,
there's no trees.

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:32.260
You almost cannot
even see the wind,

00:31:32.260 --> 00:31:33.700
because there's
no grass blowing.

00:31:33.700 --> 00:31:36.550
There's no-- the
things that normally

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:39.913
indicate depth, distance,
height, are gone.

00:31:39.913 --> 00:31:41.080
It's like being on the moon.

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:44.200
And you go, like, hmm?

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:47.980
One way of making
dimensions is by walking.

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:49.090
And so I start walking.

00:31:49.090 --> 00:31:51.100
And the two mountains
in front of me, one

00:31:51.100 --> 00:31:54.075
comes closer very fast, and
one stays on the horizon.

00:31:54.075 --> 00:31:55.450
That means, oh,
the two mountains

00:31:55.450 --> 00:31:58.090
that I thought was roughly
the same size, actually,

00:31:58.090 --> 00:31:59.780
this one is a small
mountain close by,

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:01.655
and the other one is a
big mountain far away.

00:32:01.655 --> 00:32:04.160
So by walking, I can
create a sense of scale,

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:06.580
not just out there in the
landscape, but also in my body.

00:32:06.580 --> 00:32:10.600
If I don't walk, and I stop and
I look around, it's hard for me

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:12.670
to understand, again, the scale.

00:32:12.670 --> 00:32:14.590
But then there's a waterfall.

00:32:14.590 --> 00:32:17.020
I look at the waterfall and
I go and I see the water.

00:32:17.020 --> 00:32:18.850
It falls very slowly.

00:32:18.850 --> 00:32:21.400
And I say, oh, it's
a high waterfall.

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:25.360
Because water falls roughly
with the same speed.

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:28.280
And suddenly I realized
the waterfall is also

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:30.700
a sort of temporal indicator.

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:33.490
It gives space
not just the time,

00:32:33.490 --> 00:32:34.930
but it also gives you the depth.

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:36.580
I can, by looking
at the waterfall,

00:32:36.580 --> 00:32:39.280
see how long time it
takes me to walk there.

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:40.787
I can measure the
distance from here

00:32:40.787 --> 00:32:43.120
to the waterfall and the
height simply by looking at it.

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:44.140
It's very interesting.

00:32:44.140 --> 00:32:45.880
I was sitting in
the Simien mountains

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:47.980
in the northern end of
Ethiopia, sort of looking

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:49.420
into the south of Sudan.

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:53.237
And the amazing landscape
is on the roof of Africa,

00:32:53.237 --> 00:32:54.820
it's also called the
Simien mountains.

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:57.280
And you can have your legs
off the cliff like this,

00:32:57.280 --> 00:32:59.230
and there's a drop of
a mile, approximately.

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:00.340
A kilometer, [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:03.110
So, amazing winds, the birds
sort of coming up from--

00:33:03.110 --> 00:33:05.110
and you just sit there
and you just see the sun,

00:33:05.110 --> 00:33:07.300
and it's totally amazing.

00:33:07.300 --> 00:33:10.480
And I go, like, wow, look.

00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:14.800
I wonder how far away these
small mountains down there is.

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:18.040
And this has all been, I think
it's like in 3,000 meters

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:19.360
height.

00:33:19.360 --> 00:33:20.830
What is that, 10,000 feet?

00:33:20.830 --> 00:33:23.692
So and then I go and then I
look down the rim, and I see,

00:33:23.692 --> 00:33:25.150
oh, there's a
waterfall over there,

00:33:25.150 --> 00:33:27.605
and then the water
falls so slow.

00:33:27.605 --> 00:33:29.980
And I go, (GASPS)
it's really deep.

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:30.980
And I take my legs in.

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:31.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:31.480 --> 00:33:34.690
You know, so it's
very interesting

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:37.152
what water can do to space.

00:33:37.152 --> 00:33:38.360
So now, look at public space.

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:40.120
Look at American public
space, the history

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:42.070
of public space, John
Dewey, the work that

00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:45.070
has been made into making space
the place in which we meet

00:33:45.070 --> 00:33:46.120
and share and exchange.

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:48.340
And also, a public space
has a rich history.

00:33:48.340 --> 00:33:49.520
There's different versions.

00:33:49.520 --> 00:33:51.603
There's an East Coast,
West Coast, different ideas

00:33:51.603 --> 00:33:52.870
in New York and LA.

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:54.340
So lots of material rights.

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:56.830
Then there's Europe,
the idea of Europe.

00:33:56.830 --> 00:33:58.430
Southern Europe,
Northern Europe is

00:33:58.430 --> 00:33:59.980
in sort of the
Italian public space,

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:01.600
the kind of meeting
up type of space,

00:34:01.600 --> 00:34:02.683
and the Northern European.

00:34:02.683 --> 00:34:04.180
And so something
which I find very

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:08.440
interesting as a space
which is, to a great extent,

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:11.949
there to facilitate
and reflect the values

00:34:11.949 --> 00:34:13.989
that we have in our society.

00:34:13.989 --> 00:34:17.230
And to the extent that this
is a success, we can debate.

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:18.760
Because of course,
what happens is

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:21.429
that public space is, to
a great extent privatized.

00:34:21.429 --> 00:34:24.280
It is full of intentionality,
structures, power structures,

00:34:24.280 --> 00:34:26.080
political structures,
and so on where we

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:27.639
think of it as being public--

00:34:27.639 --> 00:34:30.760
meaning free-- but it
is, in fact, not free.

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:33.280
What do we do to claim it back?

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:37.300
So the waterfalls, for
me, was about suggesting

00:34:37.300 --> 00:34:41.260
ephemeral, temporal, sort
of experiential materials.

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:45.380
Like qualities indicating that
this space actually has depth,

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:48.940
especially the sort of
skyline in New York.

00:34:48.940 --> 00:34:51.659
It's the greatest skyline, but
you also kind of look at it

00:34:51.659 --> 00:34:53.781
and say, oh, it's
exactly like a postcard.

00:34:53.781 --> 00:34:55.989
So it's a little bit like
what I said about Stockholm

00:34:55.989 --> 00:34:56.800
in the beginning.

00:34:56.800 --> 00:35:00.160
We have to make sure that a
space allows for the people

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:01.300
to be in it.

00:35:01.300 --> 00:35:05.530
Sometimes a space can be so self
obsessed with its own rendering

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:08.680
that it is only
happy when rendered.

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:12.610
We need to allow space to
have enough space for people

00:35:12.610 --> 00:35:13.300
to meet.

00:35:13.300 --> 00:35:14.800
And you need a
little space to meet.

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:17.128
Like, 100 meter,
50 meter, 20 meter.

00:35:17.128 --> 00:35:19.420
Then at 50 meter, you can
say, oh, it's a man or woman.

00:35:19.420 --> 00:35:21.070
20 meter, you can see
whether they're smiling.

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:23.470
10 meter you can can tell,
oh, better I should move out,

00:35:23.470 --> 00:35:24.760
I should go to the person.

00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:26.480
That space is important.

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:30.430
This is the space in which
we build trust in each other.

00:35:30.430 --> 00:35:33.640
If you don't have that
space, and if there's always

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:36.882
kind of a store
reaching out hey, kid,

00:35:36.882 --> 00:35:37.840
we want your attention.

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:41.315
If we don't have enough space
for us to sort of also see,

00:35:41.315 --> 00:35:42.590
[SIGHS] how do I feel?

00:35:42.590 --> 00:35:43.510
Do I feel OK?

00:35:43.510 --> 00:35:45.910
Does this space
actually support my idea

00:35:45.910 --> 00:35:48.640
of going over to the
person who fell on the bike

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:50.050
and helping the person?

00:35:50.050 --> 00:35:52.000
Or does the space say,
oh, don't-- just get,

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:55.540
just put on your Walkman
and just go, just go on.

00:35:55.540 --> 00:35:57.020
So very interesting,
these things.

00:35:57.020 --> 00:35:58.960
So the resources,
besides me being obsessed

00:35:58.960 --> 00:36:01.120
about Iceland and waterfalls
and all of that thing,

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:02.260
I think this is--

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:06.267
so for me, it was about that,
and asking these questions.

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:11.820
This is in Denmark.

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:14.280
A museum, art museum.

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:17.040
And I proposed them to
put a rainbow on the roof,

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:20.400
like a permanent rainbow.

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:21.773
So that idea is still around.

00:36:21.773 --> 00:36:22.440
Do you remember?

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:24.280
And here you have a rainbow.

00:36:24.280 --> 00:36:27.300
If you stand in one place
and just do nothing,

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:28.840
nothing changes.

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:31.680
You stand in the red area,
everything's just red.

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:33.370
Which is not so bad, also.

00:36:33.370 --> 00:36:35.870
But if you walk--

00:36:35.870 --> 00:36:38.630
which is also, if you work--

00:36:38.630 --> 00:36:39.750
then things happens.

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:41.990
If you walk around,
the color changes.

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:45.050
This means that
the walking itself

00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:47.780
is what creates here
the work of art.

00:36:47.780 --> 00:36:49.490
What people do is that
they come up here.

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:52.190
It's a lovely walk,
because you kind of go

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:53.300
through all the colors.

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:56.510
And it's, as you can tell,
it's really a very basic idea.

00:36:56.510 --> 00:36:59.810
And a lot of things happens
because the museum, of course,

00:36:59.810 --> 00:37:02.798
got very excited because a lot
of people who normally did not

00:37:02.798 --> 00:37:05.090
come to the museum suddenly
started going to the museum

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:07.220
saying, wow, this is art.

00:37:07.220 --> 00:37:09.410
It is also about
looking at the world.

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:13.010
It is about the perspective
of looking, right?

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:14.660
So we talk about [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:14.660 --> 00:37:17.390
And suddenly the
museum downstairs,

00:37:17.390 --> 00:37:19.270
the distance between
the two paintings

00:37:19.270 --> 00:37:20.270
became very interesting.

00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:22.520
You look at a painting,
a great Munch painting,

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:25.430
they have amazing
Munch paintings.

00:37:25.430 --> 00:37:28.430
But maybe the distance from
one Munch painting to the next

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:29.420
is also interesting.

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:31.190
The time it takes
for you to digest

00:37:31.190 --> 00:37:35.030
the narrative you just took in
and how that produces the next.

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:37.970
So the sequence of the
temporal aspects of a museum

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:40.280
is, of course, where
the potential lays.

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:43.430
It's not like I looked
at a Munch painting

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:45.020
and then I'm off.

00:37:45.020 --> 00:37:48.050
And then, boom, again I
look at a Munch painting.

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:50.270
The success of a
museum is, of course,

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:52.430
the kind of temporal
narrative that people

00:37:52.430 --> 00:37:53.720
take with them home.

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:55.400
We don't remember moments.

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:57.890
We remember pieces of time.

00:38:03.240 --> 00:38:04.340
The concert house.

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:09.680
So you can say I got more
involved with architecture.

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:12.290
Crystalline structures,
mathematical systems.

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:15.050
This is a quasi crystalline
pentagonal principle

00:38:15.050 --> 00:38:18.290
brought into a kind of all
space filling principle.

00:38:18.290 --> 00:38:20.420
All space filling is
when you stack stuff

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:22.510
without any cracks between them.

00:38:22.510 --> 00:38:26.520
So some time ago, I was
lucky to have an engineer

00:38:26.520 --> 00:38:29.360
sort of think about who comes
out of visionary mathematics.

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:32.330
In my studio, I occupied
him for a while,

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:35.330
and he was obsessed
with all space filling

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:36.890
mathematical principles.

00:38:36.890 --> 00:38:38.060
Stackable.

00:38:38.060 --> 00:38:40.860
Everything was stackable.

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:44.210
This, as you can see,
is a stackable facade.

00:38:44.210 --> 00:38:47.180
But crystalline and acrylic
glass and heat and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:38:47.180 --> 00:38:50.330
Also a few questions of
climate and insulation

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:52.370
and technical solutions.

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:55.100
What is maybe most interesting
is what you see here.

00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:58.130
Because if you're into
sort of facade structures

00:38:58.130 --> 00:39:00.638
and you know a big steel
facade like that, then

00:39:00.638 --> 00:39:02.180
you would know,
well, normally, there

00:39:02.180 --> 00:39:05.240
would be a column holding it.

00:39:05.240 --> 00:39:08.340
But as you can tell, it
just stands all by itself.

00:39:08.340 --> 00:39:10.610
This means it's a self
carrying structure,

00:39:10.610 --> 00:39:13.430
and so there is a bit of
engineering-- a bit of science,

00:39:13.430 --> 00:39:19.340
if you want-- into making this
actually aesthetically active.

00:39:19.340 --> 00:39:21.630
So that's a field that I've
been quite interested in.

00:39:21.630 --> 00:39:25.700
So it's not an existing building
on which I apply a work of art

00:39:25.700 --> 00:39:29.060
as kind of decorating, painting
on something already there.

00:39:29.060 --> 00:39:31.718
It is a part of the
building structure.

00:39:31.718 --> 00:39:33.260
So when the architect
asked me to get

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:35.540
involved because they needed
to win the competition,

00:39:35.540 --> 00:39:36.440
I said, OK.

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:39.585
But I want to do something that
actually holds up the building.

00:39:39.585 --> 00:39:41.960
I don't want to do something
that decorates the building.

00:39:41.960 --> 00:39:43.377
If I'm not a part
of the building,

00:39:43.377 --> 00:39:45.530
I won't be a part of the team.

00:39:45.530 --> 00:39:50.630
Which of course,
was not a problem.

00:39:50.630 --> 00:39:54.050
This is the model, my models.

00:39:54.050 --> 00:39:56.540
This is, I've been looking
a lot into mathematical

00:39:56.540 --> 00:40:00.290
and natural scientific systems
of space depiction and spatial

00:40:00.290 --> 00:40:01.430
processes and so on.

00:40:01.430 --> 00:40:06.200
I won't go into
that too much here.

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:07.730
The evolution of building.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:14.260
This is actually, this
is quite a while ago

00:40:14.260 --> 00:40:17.830
when the wall in
Berlin came down,

00:40:17.830 --> 00:40:22.060
a few years did pass by before
the walls of Albania came down.

00:40:22.060 --> 00:40:25.420
I was very interested in
Albania when it came down,

00:40:25.420 --> 00:40:28.810
because Albania was so
close, and the city of Tirana

00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:30.880
was just in the
center of Europe,

00:40:30.880 --> 00:40:34.330
but it had not been accessible
as we all know and so on.

00:40:34.330 --> 00:40:38.930
So quite early on after
Albania becoming open,

00:40:38.930 --> 00:40:40.930
there was a number of
architectural competitions

00:40:40.930 --> 00:40:41.950
in which I was involved.

00:40:41.950 --> 00:40:45.790
And the mayor, Edi
Rama of Tirana,

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:48.310
invited me to be on a
sort of advisory board

00:40:48.310 --> 00:40:49.060
for city planning.

00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:52.640
And I was, not so long after
I was not involved anymore.

00:40:52.640 --> 00:40:59.060
But the point here is that I
started looking into, well,

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:01.750
how do we hand over the
ownership of the space

00:41:01.750 --> 00:41:04.510
to the people who were
deprived from thinking

00:41:04.510 --> 00:41:06.580
that they could produce space?

00:41:06.580 --> 00:41:09.940
And one of the process
was to let's make

00:41:09.940 --> 00:41:11.680
a city plan out of LEGO bricks.

00:41:11.680 --> 00:41:15.070
And I called Kjeld Kirk,
the owner of LEGO, said,

00:41:15.070 --> 00:41:16.817
we need some LEGO
bricks in Tirana.

00:41:16.817 --> 00:41:19.150
And he was very interested,
because the people had never

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:20.250
played with LEGO.

00:41:20.250 --> 00:41:21.250
That's very interesting.

00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:22.840
It's almost impossible.

00:41:22.840 --> 00:41:24.940
And now there's,
like, no one left.

00:41:24.940 --> 00:41:28.720
But so I said, let's
put in a big sort

00:41:28.720 --> 00:41:31.192
of a table, the same
size as the kind of,

00:41:31.192 --> 00:41:32.650
sort of roughly
depicting the city.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:36.310
And then let's allow people
to go, and then build.

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:39.700
So a few things interesting.

00:41:39.700 --> 00:41:45.188
People started territorially
thinking about, well,

00:41:45.188 --> 00:41:47.230
should I be, or should it
be next to this person?

00:41:47.230 --> 00:41:50.350
And the kind of policemen from
the city, the kids selling

00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:52.637
cigarettes out of this
kind of [INAUDIBLE],,

00:41:52.637 --> 00:41:54.220
they were standing
next to each other.

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:55.780
At some point, the gun
from the policeman's,

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:57.940
like, on the table, and
the cigarettes of the kid

00:41:57.940 --> 00:41:58.780
was on this table.

00:41:58.780 --> 00:42:00.070
It was very interesting.

00:42:00.070 --> 00:42:03.250
And not that it was like kind
of a revolution or anything,

00:42:03.250 --> 00:42:08.100
but there was this kind of
coming together on a project.

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:11.190
Details, a lot of the
buildings had the LEGO brick

00:42:11.190 --> 00:42:12.150
the other way around.

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:15.420
Like with the sort of
round things going down.

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:18.730
Because nobody had
actually necessarily built.

00:42:18.730 --> 00:42:23.140
And if you can see, they didn't
press them very well together.

00:42:23.140 --> 00:42:24.180
Right?

00:42:24.180 --> 00:42:27.150
So we grew up-- and
I don't mean this,

00:42:27.150 --> 00:42:28.360
it's not like we and them.

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:30.270
But by all means, we--

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:33.090
it's just so interesting
that the kind of knowledge

00:42:33.090 --> 00:42:35.560
that we have in our hands
pressing a LEGO brick together,

00:42:35.560 --> 00:42:36.060
and so on.

00:42:36.060 --> 00:42:38.400
So the LEGO owner, he
was actually there,

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:41.687
standing there, looking.

00:42:41.687 --> 00:42:44.020
And he was so surprised they
wouldn't press it together.

00:42:44.020 --> 00:42:44.740
He's an engineer.

00:42:44.740 --> 00:42:45.407
So he said, why?

00:42:45.407 --> 00:42:46.810
I wonder why people don't--

00:42:46.810 --> 00:42:47.590
why?

00:42:47.590 --> 00:42:50.420
And it was so interesting
seeing him looking at that.

00:42:50.420 --> 00:42:52.900
And so, lots of lovely details.

00:42:52.900 --> 00:42:55.852
But really, also about, what
does it mean to share a space?

00:42:55.852 --> 00:42:57.310
What does it mean--
and, of course,

00:42:57.310 --> 00:42:59.680
some people immediately
build a big wall

00:42:59.680 --> 00:43:00.680
and a fence around them.

00:43:00.680 --> 00:43:03.290
But some people started making
bridges and breaking down

00:43:03.290 --> 00:43:03.790
the walls.

00:43:08.270 --> 00:43:11.692
Oh, I have to do like this.

00:43:11.692 --> 00:43:13.400
You sort of saw that
in the introduction.

00:43:13.400 --> 00:43:15.480
I won't drag you
through that again.

00:43:15.480 --> 00:43:17.965
But-- oh, now it's
going backwards.

00:43:17.965 --> 00:43:18.590
I wonder if I--

00:43:22.860 --> 00:43:24.150
you saw it before.

00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:26.880
Point is, so sometimes,
in my studio,

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:29.500
I do these spatial experiments.

00:43:29.500 --> 00:43:33.240
Some of them requires dancers
to bring the body back

00:43:33.240 --> 00:43:34.140
into my work.

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:36.550
Then I record it and film
and make a little work up out

00:43:36.550 --> 00:43:37.050
of it.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:39.090
But the interesting
part is really the idea,

00:43:39.090 --> 00:43:42.030
if you can walk very
slowly, and then you

00:43:42.030 --> 00:43:44.460
have the kind of slow
motion trainer to come.

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:46.140
And then we practice
in the studio.

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:49.080
Not that I need people to
be slow, but interestingly,

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:52.820
everything else gets
proportionally fast.

00:43:52.820 --> 00:43:55.530
So I tried this.

00:43:55.530 --> 00:43:56.237
Oh.

00:43:56.237 --> 00:43:58.320
I wonder whether I have a
slide where I'm walking?

00:43:58.320 --> 00:43:59.130
No, I don't.

00:43:59.130 --> 00:43:59.670
OK.

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:01.970
So anyway-- is it there?

00:44:01.970 --> 00:44:04.440
Is it coming?

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:07.960
If I press here?

00:44:07.960 --> 00:44:08.870
Is it after?

00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:09.370
Later?

00:44:09.370 --> 00:44:09.940
OK, anyway.

00:44:09.940 --> 00:44:11.580
So it's coming up.

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:16.000
It's very difficult
to investigate time.

00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:19.720
Obviously, time plays
a role in my works.

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:22.780
Oh, this is actually
a little film.

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:25.930
But the one I think you saw,
also, in the introduction.

00:44:25.930 --> 00:44:29.680
But time being kind of a
coproducer of the works--

00:44:29.680 --> 00:44:32.220
I'll just go on, I'm sorry.

00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:35.680
Time being a coproducer
of the work is, I think,

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:37.410
very difficult,
but very important.

00:44:37.410 --> 00:44:39.090
Because time is so
intangible, it's

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:43.340
difficult to control
and make explicit.

00:44:43.340 --> 00:44:46.010
It does take time
to build confidence.

00:44:46.010 --> 00:44:48.200
It does take time to
build self-confidence.

00:44:48.200 --> 00:44:49.890
It takes time to experience.

00:44:49.890 --> 00:44:53.210
So this means we do in the
studio where I occupy around

00:44:53.210 --> 00:44:58.340
90 people, we do make
some effort in exercising,

00:44:58.340 --> 00:45:02.690
how does the passing or the
flowing of time actually feel?

00:45:02.690 --> 00:45:05.570
I should ask you, Margaret.

00:45:05.570 --> 00:45:07.040
By all means.

00:45:07.040 --> 00:45:08.600
It's just so important
that we don't

00:45:08.600 --> 00:45:13.250
forget how the experience
builds the moment

00:45:13.250 --> 00:45:14.780
and turns into memories.

00:45:14.780 --> 00:45:17.480
So I work a lot with
this thing, and I

00:45:17.480 --> 00:45:20.240
make an effort to
bring some communal,

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:22.940
let's say, projects
into the studio where we

00:45:22.940 --> 00:45:25.280
invite people to come and eat.

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:28.760
We have a few lovely
chefs cooking every day.

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:31.130
And the 90 people is,
this is the moment where

00:45:31.130 --> 00:45:34.130
the three teams in the studio,
the architectural teams,

00:45:34.130 --> 00:45:35.990
the craftsmen who
works with their hands

00:45:35.990 --> 00:45:39.770
and the archive, the
research and archive people,

00:45:39.770 --> 00:45:40.400
they all meet.

00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:44.540
And they exchange, you know,
formalities and informalities.

00:45:44.540 --> 00:45:46.310
And often we have
friends like, you

00:45:46.310 --> 00:45:49.700
can just see this is Rirkrit
down here in the corner,

00:45:49.700 --> 00:45:53.470
grabbing, sneaking
in and having a bite.

00:45:53.470 --> 00:45:55.940
The studio has-- if you
don't mind me walking down

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:58.220
the path of the studio a
little bit-- the studio has

00:45:58.220 --> 00:45:59.900
on the top floor, that's
the red building there

00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:01.430
on the left side,
it's the studio.

00:46:01.430 --> 00:46:04.070
On the upper floor,
it has a school.

00:46:04.070 --> 00:46:06.950
It had a school which
closed two weeks ago, where

00:46:06.950 --> 00:46:10.010
I, for five years, under
the funding of the Science

00:46:10.010 --> 00:46:14.810
Ministry in Berlin, were looking
into education and methodology

00:46:14.810 --> 00:46:16.980
in art education.

00:46:16.980 --> 00:46:20.780
I know Molly and a few friends
from here has also been there.

00:46:20.780 --> 00:46:23.750
And we really tried to
build a system which

00:46:23.750 --> 00:46:28.400
was driven by non-curriculum
education, where we took

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:30.080
for granted something
very important--

00:46:30.080 --> 00:46:32.630
that the students were already
creative by the time they made

00:46:32.630 --> 00:46:33.628
it into the school.

00:46:33.628 --> 00:46:35.420
So I didn't have to
teach them to make art.

00:46:35.420 --> 00:46:39.230
I was there to help them to
bring thinking into doing.

00:46:39.230 --> 00:46:42.050
And also having confidence
with abstraction.

00:46:42.050 --> 00:46:44.970
Something that is less
tangible, not quantifiable.

00:46:44.970 --> 00:46:47.570
The success could
be in the temporal

00:46:47.570 --> 00:46:50.810
and not in the sort of
solidification of the idea.

00:46:50.810 --> 00:46:53.790
And the school brought about
it a lot of experiments.

00:46:53.790 --> 00:46:56.210
So one experiment,
as you can see,

00:46:56.210 --> 00:46:58.610
this is a flag,
kind of flagpole.

00:46:58.610 --> 00:47:01.100
But the flag itself
is a piece of metal.

00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:04.940
When the wind blowed, it was
like, it was like thunder.

00:47:04.940 --> 00:47:06.470
It was a lovely student project.

00:47:06.470 --> 00:47:09.590
So it's like the
thundering flag.

00:47:09.590 --> 00:47:11.420
We took that down
to Addis Ababa where

00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:13.220
I spent two months
with the studio also

00:47:13.220 --> 00:47:17.810
teaching and collaborating
with the Addis Ababa University

00:47:17.810 --> 00:47:19.190
and the department there.

00:47:19.190 --> 00:47:21.120
And the thundering
flag of course,

00:47:21.120 --> 00:47:23.090
meant something very
different in Ethiopia

00:47:23.090 --> 00:47:24.830
than it does in Berlin.

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:29.140
Not too different, though.

00:47:31.960 --> 00:47:33.160
Another flag.

00:47:33.160 --> 00:47:36.940
So all the students, it sounds
like they only do flags,

00:47:36.940 --> 00:47:39.310
but this is like a giant
flag that you sort of walk

00:47:39.310 --> 00:47:40.450
through the city.

00:47:40.450 --> 00:47:42.700
Or the idea of taking
away the eyes, sort

00:47:42.700 --> 00:47:44.020
of getting a sense of space.

00:47:44.020 --> 00:47:47.350
And student experiments
where physicality,

00:47:47.350 --> 00:47:51.940
the doing component, was
maybe more in focus than the--

00:47:51.940 --> 00:47:53.530
I wouldn't say
we're not thinking.

00:47:53.530 --> 00:47:57.780
But thinking alone, for sure,
won't make you an artist.

00:47:57.780 --> 00:48:01.110
Walking through the city
using a reflected image.

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:06.195
It's actually very difficult,
but an interesting exercise.

00:48:06.195 --> 00:48:08.070
Because you have to kind
of work a little bit

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:10.310
on the construction
of how you see.

00:48:10.310 --> 00:48:12.870
And suddenly, you
can make new rules.

00:48:15.750 --> 00:48:18.750
Motoric, you know, frictions.

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:20.550
So taking away
the basic motoric,

00:48:20.550 --> 00:48:22.920
just making the cutlery
like a meter long.

00:48:22.920 --> 00:48:24.390
It's very difficult to--

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:24.930
see.

00:48:24.930 --> 00:48:26.390
Where you kind of--

00:48:26.390 --> 00:48:28.350
also spatial.

00:48:28.350 --> 00:48:31.130
The famous studio
lioning experiment.

00:48:31.130 --> 00:48:33.063
So we're trying to
train to be a lion.

00:48:33.063 --> 00:48:34.980
We did that last night
up in the Media Lounge.

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:37.110
Any lions from last night here?

00:48:37.110 --> 00:48:38.220
We were all--

00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:39.900
Geoffrey.

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:42.690
So we were, it's my fourth
lecture, as I told you.

00:48:42.690 --> 00:48:45.330
A lovely lecture last night
with the tickling laughter

00:48:45.330 --> 00:48:48.180
and the lion experiment.

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:53.700
To lion, is, of course,
pretending to be a lion.

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:55.380
Once you start to
pretend to be a lion,

00:48:55.380 --> 00:48:56.790
you realize, oh
I could actually,

00:48:56.790 --> 00:48:58.082
I could actually get into that.

00:48:58.082 --> 00:48:59.370
I could get very good at it.

00:48:59.370 --> 00:49:02.190
And to become a lion,
it is not so easy,

00:49:02.190 --> 00:49:04.680
but it's also not impossible.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:06.790
And you start with a paw.

00:49:06.790 --> 00:49:09.340
As you can-- and it takes,
like, we won't do it now.

00:49:09.340 --> 00:49:12.570
But you can sort of,
it's called pawing.

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:15.360
The pawing exercise is
already in your mind.

00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:16.920
There's a little
space in your brain

00:49:16.920 --> 00:49:19.800
that start to evolve, oh,
how do I make a lion paw?

00:49:19.800 --> 00:49:22.110
So two cushions,
you're like a lion.

00:49:22.110 --> 00:49:24.780
This sort of nice little
four toes on the front.

00:49:24.780 --> 00:49:28.510
You can kind of, like, you can
start to the pawing exercise.

00:49:28.510 --> 00:49:30.690
And if you go through the
whole thing, you get lion.

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:34.830
And then there's the
famous Sphinx down here.

00:49:34.830 --> 00:49:38.490
It's a French, it's a French--

00:49:38.490 --> 00:49:39.660
I can talk about it.

00:49:39.660 --> 00:49:43.290
The lion experiment comes out
of the French conceptual dancer

00:49:43.290 --> 00:49:46.950
who, you might know, had a
lot of success in the '90s,

00:49:46.950 --> 00:49:48.482
called Xavier Le Roy.

00:49:48.482 --> 00:49:49.190
Is that his name?

00:49:49.190 --> 00:49:50.070
Xavier Le Roy?

00:49:50.070 --> 00:49:53.190
Amazing French dancer,
conceptual dancer,

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:57.150
who made a lot of
physical experiments,

00:49:57.150 --> 00:49:58.650
like one does in dance.

00:49:58.650 --> 00:50:01.620
But turning the studio
into a Savannah.

00:50:01.620 --> 00:50:05.850
So a very healthy exercise.

00:50:05.850 --> 00:50:08.313
See, that's a
classic lion posture.

00:50:08.313 --> 00:50:11.211
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:15.080 --> 00:50:16.790
Our slow motion trainer.

00:50:16.790 --> 00:50:18.350
That guy on that photo.

00:50:18.350 --> 00:50:23.630
Now you'll see I'm coming
closer to round this up,

00:50:23.630 --> 00:50:25.130
talking about Little Sun.

00:50:25.130 --> 00:50:27.230
I wonder, do we
have a Little Sun?

00:50:27.230 --> 00:50:30.140
Frederick, do you
have a Little Sun?

00:50:30.140 --> 00:50:30.640
Oh, yeah.

00:50:33.340 --> 00:50:34.230
Good.

00:50:34.230 --> 00:50:39.750
So this is our, I went to
China outside of Shanghai,

00:50:39.750 --> 00:50:43.530
in the [INAUDIBLE] gardens.

00:50:43.530 --> 00:50:47.190
Amazing rock gardens with
amazing architecture.

00:50:47.190 --> 00:50:52.402
And I made a film
about time and space.

00:50:52.402 --> 00:50:53.860
And these are the
gardens that went

00:50:53.860 --> 00:50:57.880
before the Japanese gardens,
kind of the ancestor

00:50:57.880 --> 00:50:59.400
to the Japanese gardens.

00:50:59.400 --> 00:51:00.400
And they're lovely here.

00:51:00.400 --> 00:51:04.460
It's winter, so
there's no leaves.

00:51:04.460 --> 00:51:06.400
Very minimal.

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:12.250
And Steen, who is an old street
dancer, now a slow motion

00:51:12.250 --> 00:51:12.940
specialist.

00:51:15.890 --> 00:51:18.610
So it was about, so public,
again, next to the street,

00:51:18.610 --> 00:51:20.870
and to kind of--

00:51:20.870 --> 00:51:23.270
as you know, in China, they
have all these round doors,

00:51:23.270 --> 00:51:26.400
and I had these
mirrors in the film.

00:51:26.400 --> 00:51:29.550
And the film is 20 minutes long.

00:51:29.550 --> 00:51:30.050
So.

00:51:36.070 --> 00:51:39.958
It's just that one
shot, or is it--

00:51:39.958 --> 00:51:41.390
ah, just see the next scene.

00:51:47.260 --> 00:51:49.015
Here Steen is treeing.

00:51:56.020 --> 00:51:57.330
See the tree on one side?

00:52:01.320 --> 00:52:02.340
I can't.

00:52:02.340 --> 00:52:03.630
It's really funny, I think.

00:52:03.630 --> 00:52:05.830
But anyway, so this is me
just bringing it to him.

00:52:05.830 --> 00:52:09.630
And so I actually do, to get
inspired, I sometimes go out.

00:52:09.630 --> 00:52:11.530
This is the park
close to my studio.

00:52:11.530 --> 00:52:15.360
And then I'm not as good
at staying in slow motion,

00:52:15.360 --> 00:52:16.930
but I go out and I train.

00:52:16.930 --> 00:52:19.600
Doing that in public spaces,
like also investigating

00:52:19.600 --> 00:52:22.440
the elasticity of
the public space,

00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:24.660
does the public
space really like

00:52:24.660 --> 00:52:26.970
me making slow motion in it?

00:52:26.970 --> 00:52:28.722
You know?

00:52:28.722 --> 00:52:30.930
Look at that lovely woman
there, how she looks at me,

00:52:30.930 --> 00:52:33.550
and she goes, what
is this guy doing?

00:52:33.550 --> 00:52:34.050
You know?

00:52:38.100 --> 00:52:41.850
But the question is really
not her looking at me.

00:52:41.850 --> 00:52:44.500
The question is more,
how does she feel?

00:52:44.500 --> 00:52:46.860
Does she feel comfortable
or uncomfortable?

00:52:46.860 --> 00:52:48.720
But just because I'm
walking slow motion,

00:52:48.720 --> 00:52:50.670
does she kind of
hold on to her bag?

00:52:50.670 --> 00:52:51.810
You know?

00:52:51.810 --> 00:52:56.760
So we have to ask ourselves, the
spaces in which we move, what

00:52:56.760 --> 00:52:58.830
feelings do they facilitate?

00:52:58.830 --> 00:53:00.690
What type of
artistic intervention

00:53:00.690 --> 00:53:05.000
would maybe make that explicit,
or challenge that, even?

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:07.610
Is it a problem that
people move differently?

00:53:07.610 --> 00:53:09.260
Or maybe it's good.

00:53:09.260 --> 00:53:13.215
Maybe diversifying
movements is interesting.

00:53:17.020 --> 00:53:19.000
Do you mind me just
skipping that picture?

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:20.470
And this one?

00:53:20.470 --> 00:53:22.040
And going to this one.

00:53:22.040 --> 00:53:23.860
Because I think I
spoke for a while.

00:53:23.860 --> 00:53:24.880
I'll just grab a little.

00:53:30.860 --> 00:53:36.200
So a few years from now, a
father from my son's class--

00:53:36.200 --> 00:53:39.470
or a guy who has a son in
the same class as my son--

00:53:39.470 --> 00:53:43.520
and I were talking, and it turns
out he was a solar engineer.

00:53:43.520 --> 00:53:46.070
And knew a lot about light.

00:53:46.070 --> 00:53:48.860
And I'm obsessed with
light, and the kind

00:53:48.860 --> 00:53:50.690
of ephemeral quality of light.

00:53:50.690 --> 00:53:54.920
And, you know, the
sun, as you have seen.

00:53:54.920 --> 00:53:57.170
And suddenly we had--

00:53:57.170 --> 00:54:01.640
to make a long story short-- we
had this idea of storing light,

00:54:01.640 --> 00:54:03.050
storing energy.

00:54:03.050 --> 00:54:07.280
And if you could go out into the
sun and kind of take some sun,

00:54:07.280 --> 00:54:10.520
just like, do like this,
and then record the sun,

00:54:10.520 --> 00:54:12.500
almost like sample.

00:54:12.500 --> 00:54:13.820
Like a DJ makes a sample.

00:54:13.820 --> 00:54:15.770
And then you take
it somewhere else,

00:54:15.770 --> 00:54:19.010
and then you say, OK, this is
the light, I can show you here.

00:54:19.010 --> 00:54:22.280
This is how the sun
in Berlin looks like.

00:54:22.280 --> 00:54:23.750
This is sunlight from Berlin.

00:54:23.750 --> 00:54:27.350
We recorded the light with this
one, the little black thing

00:54:27.350 --> 00:54:29.968
here, and I'm playing
it here in Boston.

00:54:29.968 --> 00:54:31.010
So it's very interesting.

00:54:31.010 --> 00:54:34.340
The light from Berlin traveled,
so the ephemeral quality

00:54:34.340 --> 00:54:37.340
of the light from Berlin
traveled with me on the plane

00:54:37.340 --> 00:54:39.110
and came to Boston.

00:54:39.110 --> 00:54:42.260
And of course, what that means
is that I have a little power

00:54:42.260 --> 00:54:43.160
station.

00:54:43.160 --> 00:54:49.040
I have access to power in
terms of decentralized power.

00:54:49.040 --> 00:54:50.750
I have my own power station.

00:54:50.750 --> 00:54:53.300
I am my own power station.

00:54:53.300 --> 00:54:57.650
I can choose to have
energy that is mine.

00:54:57.650 --> 00:54:59.780
I chart not just
the power station.

00:54:59.780 --> 00:55:01.530
I chart myself.

00:55:01.530 --> 00:55:03.440
So it's very interesting
with regards to,

00:55:03.440 --> 00:55:06.990
do I feel resourceful or not?

00:55:06.990 --> 00:55:10.200
Do the space in which I am
allow me to feel resourceful?

00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:11.340
What type of space am I?

00:55:11.340 --> 00:55:14.340
Maybe the power in
this space I am.

00:55:14.340 --> 00:55:18.630
It's highly, let's
say, centralized.

00:55:18.630 --> 00:55:23.140
Maybe I have to support
the oil industry.

00:55:23.140 --> 00:55:26.530
So maybe the power is
really not the power I want.

00:55:26.530 --> 00:55:28.290
Maybe I want this power instead.

00:55:28.290 --> 00:55:31.830
If I'm a child who grows up
reading my bedtime stories

00:55:31.830 --> 00:55:35.280
with this light,
from six and so on,

00:55:35.280 --> 00:55:36.900
maybe I'm more
likely, once I have

00:55:36.900 --> 00:55:39.360
to make the choice for energy
in my house when I grow up,

00:55:39.360 --> 00:55:42.930
maybe I'm more likely to have
confidence in solar energy

00:55:42.930 --> 00:55:45.620
or in alternative
energy resources.

00:55:45.620 --> 00:55:49.290
There's so much material in this
which is about so many things.

00:55:49.290 --> 00:55:51.630
Frederick Ottesen, the engineer
who's sitting over here,

00:55:51.630 --> 00:55:54.290
you should talk to him,
because he's really,

00:55:54.290 --> 00:55:57.870
he's really very
MIT, if you want.

00:55:57.870 --> 00:56:01.530
The confidence in this
was, to a great extent,

00:56:01.530 --> 00:56:04.110
inspired by the collaboration
between engineering

00:56:04.110 --> 00:56:05.370
and creativity.

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:09.190
The need, of course, is to raise
the question, the awareness,

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:11.010
the policymaking
about, what does it

00:56:11.010 --> 00:56:13.140
mean to have access to energy?

00:56:13.140 --> 00:56:15.600
And who have that
responsibility?

00:56:15.600 --> 00:56:17.700
And we very quickly came
to the conclusion, well,

00:56:17.700 --> 00:56:19.230
this has to work for everybody.

00:56:19.230 --> 00:56:22.840
It is not about off grid.

00:56:22.840 --> 00:56:24.850
It's about every grid.

00:56:24.850 --> 00:56:26.620
Both off and on grid.

00:56:26.620 --> 00:56:29.557
Of course, this story about
the climate that I was talking

00:56:29.557 --> 00:56:31.390
about, suddenly you
have a little thing here

00:56:31.390 --> 00:56:33.910
that makes something
a little bit explicit.

00:56:33.910 --> 00:56:37.090
Let's just say it's a foot
in the door in understanding

00:56:37.090 --> 00:56:38.470
what on earth is energy.

00:56:38.470 --> 00:56:38.997
Right?

00:56:38.997 --> 00:56:41.080
So it normally just comes
out of a plug somewhere,

00:56:41.080 --> 00:56:42.163
and then you just take it.

00:56:42.163 --> 00:56:43.840
And we sometimes
make the mistake,

00:56:43.840 --> 00:56:46.240
we take the energy for
granted, as if it was just

00:56:46.240 --> 00:56:47.743
like kind of in abundance.

00:56:47.743 --> 00:56:49.660
To understand that energy,
you have to go out,

00:56:49.660 --> 00:56:50.618
you have to collect it.

00:56:50.618 --> 00:56:53.170
And to make, to have a
little power station sort of

00:56:53.170 --> 00:56:58.190
teaches you, just
like when, what

00:56:58.190 --> 00:57:01.210
do we call this, when we got
the little first steam machine.

00:57:01.210 --> 00:57:02.930
Like, how amazing is
that little thing?

00:57:02.930 --> 00:57:03.940
Where you're boiling
away your water,

00:57:03.940 --> 00:57:05.732
and then suddenly the
wheel turns, and then

00:57:05.732 --> 00:57:08.000
you have energy, like,
boiling the water.

00:57:08.000 --> 00:57:10.270
So this is the
kind of same thing.

00:57:10.270 --> 00:57:12.190
Of course, we then
went specifically

00:57:12.190 --> 00:57:16.270
to address the need for
energy in certain groups.

00:57:16.270 --> 00:57:18.070
We are looking
now at rural parts

00:57:18.070 --> 00:57:20.830
of the world where there is no
energy access, such as Africa.

00:57:20.830 --> 00:57:22.150
We have worked for two years.

00:57:22.150 --> 00:57:24.858
We're on the ground in
Africa in eight countries.

00:57:24.858 --> 00:57:26.650
We're selling them
through a different sort

00:57:26.650 --> 00:57:29.080
of private,
market-driven, NGO-driven.

00:57:29.080 --> 00:57:31.120
Also with a market component.

00:57:31.120 --> 00:57:33.550
It's not an aid project,
and some governments

00:57:33.550 --> 00:57:34.870
are totally not successful.

00:57:34.870 --> 00:57:36.495
Working with governments
is impossible.

00:57:36.495 --> 00:57:38.500
But, you know, the other people.

00:57:38.500 --> 00:57:40.720
This is a great success
in terms of where

00:57:40.720 --> 00:57:44.840
we are seeing fantastic
sales in the world,

00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:48.190
whether it's energy
at a higher price--

00:57:48.190 --> 00:57:50.350
$20, $30.

00:57:50.350 --> 00:57:52.463
Actually, MoMA in
New York is next week

00:57:52.463 --> 00:57:54.880
launching their Spring Catalog
where this is on the cover.

00:57:54.880 --> 00:57:57.213
So a lot of lamps and
so on and so forth.

00:57:57.213 --> 00:57:58.630
We're selling them
here in Boston.

00:57:58.630 --> 00:58:00.790
Also, we have
started selling them

00:58:00.790 --> 00:58:03.880
on museums and online and
design stores and so on.

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:07.000
With that profit,
we deliver the lamps

00:58:07.000 --> 00:58:10.420
in outlets which also
sell them to build

00:58:10.420 --> 00:58:12.880
local economies, micro
economies, and sort

00:58:12.880 --> 00:58:16.180
of street vendor economies,
informal economies.

00:58:16.180 --> 00:58:19.030
And by doing so, we hope to
create sort of enough profit

00:58:19.030 --> 00:58:22.090
at the sort of end of the, what
do you call it, the last mile,

00:58:22.090 --> 00:58:24.070
in order for them
to order more lamps.

00:58:24.070 --> 00:58:26.570
So it's really not just
about somebody resourceful

00:58:26.570 --> 00:58:27.820
and somebody less resourceful.

00:58:27.820 --> 00:58:30.790
It's about sort of sharing
a vision of distributing

00:58:30.790 --> 00:58:34.760
the resources accordingly.

00:58:34.760 --> 00:58:37.900
But the core of this
is a lot of the things

00:58:37.900 --> 00:58:40.030
that I spoke about
in the other project.

00:58:40.030 --> 00:58:42.700
This is a work of art that
then has wandered outside.

00:58:42.700 --> 00:58:44.200
And sometimes, for
a lot of people,

00:58:44.200 --> 00:58:46.780
it doesn't necessarily
mean, or doesn't necessarily

00:58:46.780 --> 00:58:48.400
have to be a work of art.

00:58:48.400 --> 00:58:50.170
But it's never just functional.

00:58:50.170 --> 00:58:52.590
It's never just
something functional.

00:58:52.590 --> 00:58:53.770
Oh, I need light.

00:58:53.770 --> 00:58:56.350
If it doesn't touch
you emotionally,

00:58:56.350 --> 00:58:59.500
if you don't feel like you're
holding hands with the sun,

00:58:59.500 --> 00:59:00.910
you won't buy it.

00:59:00.910 --> 00:59:02.530
You are not going
to convince somebody

00:59:02.530 --> 00:59:07.520
to buy a bag, a woman's bag,
[INAUDIBLE] confirm that,

00:59:07.520 --> 00:59:10.100
if it's just to carry
your stuff around in it.

00:59:10.100 --> 00:59:10.650
Right?

00:59:10.650 --> 00:59:13.040
So if that was it,
we wouldn't have

00:59:13.040 --> 00:59:16.670
all these incredible companies
now with all these brands

00:59:16.670 --> 00:59:19.500
and so on if it was all
about functionality.

00:59:19.500 --> 00:59:21.080
This is the same thing.

00:59:21.080 --> 00:59:23.280
If it doesn't inspire people.

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:25.190
I saw a young mother
taking her two kids

00:59:25.190 --> 00:59:27.080
to school walking
out in the morning,

00:59:27.080 --> 00:59:29.030
charging this lamp
around her neck.

00:59:29.030 --> 00:59:32.120
And the woman next door says,
oh, my god, look at you,

00:59:32.120 --> 00:59:32.713
look at you.

00:59:32.713 --> 00:59:34.880
You have to wear this because
you don't have energy.

00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:38.000
Oh, you are a victim.

00:59:38.000 --> 00:59:41.510
No, the woman who takes
her kids to school and they

00:59:41.510 --> 00:59:43.760
who make their homework
with this in the evening,

00:59:43.760 --> 00:59:45.990
that woman needs to
say, oh, look at me.

00:59:45.990 --> 00:59:47.990
And the woman next door
has to say, oh, amazing.

00:59:47.990 --> 00:59:49.240
Why don't I have one of these?

00:59:49.240 --> 00:59:51.470
This is cool.

00:59:51.470 --> 00:59:52.970
So there's a lot
of stories in this,

00:59:52.970 --> 00:59:56.130
but we are trying to address
the language in the same way we

00:59:56.130 --> 00:59:57.020
would do here.

00:59:57.020 --> 00:59:59.930
That this is about desire,
aspiration, inspiration.

00:59:59.930 --> 01:00:02.990
It's about creativity
and self-confidence.

01:00:02.990 --> 01:00:06.487
This is, I think, where
engineering and creativity can

01:00:06.487 --> 01:00:07.070
work together.

01:00:07.070 --> 01:00:11.300
And of course, a great
amount of the success of MIT

01:00:11.300 --> 01:00:17.000
has been in its ability to
lean out into other fields.

01:00:17.000 --> 01:00:20.060
And I would encourage on
the occasion of today,

01:00:20.060 --> 01:00:21.980
and in the spirit
of the McDermott,

01:00:21.980 --> 01:00:23.960
I would encourage
this to continue.

01:00:23.960 --> 01:00:27.620
To take into your heart the
potential of working together

01:00:27.620 --> 01:00:30.410
with, let's just say, the
cultural sector, into the arts,

01:00:30.410 --> 01:00:33.020
into the dancers, into
the people who are slow,

01:00:33.020 --> 01:00:34.610
into the people who are fast.

01:00:34.610 --> 01:00:37.250
Into the writers and theaters
and ballets and music

01:00:37.250 --> 01:00:38.190
and so on.

01:00:38.190 --> 01:00:40.920
And I think this is
the way to the future.

01:00:40.920 --> 01:00:43.100
As I said in the beginning,
the models through which

01:00:43.100 --> 01:00:44.930
we are going to create change.

01:00:44.930 --> 01:00:46.610
Of course, some
engineering is needed.

01:00:46.610 --> 01:00:48.530
I give you that, Frederick.

01:00:48.530 --> 01:00:52.622
But touching people
takes something more

01:00:52.622 --> 01:00:53.330
than [INAUDIBLE].

01:00:53.330 --> 01:00:55.490
Touching people alone
won't do it, also.

01:00:55.490 --> 01:00:58.430
So the coming together
between the thinking and the

01:00:58.430 --> 01:01:00.290
doing, thinking
being engineering,

01:01:00.290 --> 01:01:01.790
and the doing being engineering.

01:01:01.790 --> 01:01:03.457
The thinking being
also art and so on.

01:01:03.457 --> 01:01:05.540
This is, I think, one of
the solutions in which we

01:01:05.540 --> 01:01:08.820
can create a lot of change.

01:01:08.820 --> 01:01:10.530
I thank you so much for coming.

01:01:10.530 --> 01:01:11.810
Here we have a few questions.

01:01:11.810 --> 01:01:15.730
[APPLAUSE]

01:01:17.200 --> 01:01:18.180
Thank you.

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:19.160
That was fabulous.

01:01:19.160 --> 01:01:22.590
Thanks [INAUDIBLE].

01:01:22.590 --> 01:01:24.060
So you'll take some questions?

01:01:24.060 --> 01:01:25.040
Yes, of course.

01:01:29.470 --> 01:01:32.200
We have some time for
questions and comments.

01:01:32.200 --> 01:01:34.450
And I believe we have
some microphones,

01:01:34.450 --> 01:01:35.710
so we'll pass those out.

01:01:35.710 --> 01:01:40.130
And anyone want to go first?

01:01:40.130 --> 01:01:41.930
Yes, please, if you would.

01:01:41.930 --> 01:01:44.180
We need someone on this side,
someone on that-- thanks

01:01:44.180 --> 01:01:45.050
so much.

01:01:45.050 --> 01:01:46.210
I just saw a hand there.

01:01:52.940 --> 01:01:53.630
Hi.

01:01:53.630 --> 01:01:56.695
This is really loud.

01:01:56.695 --> 01:01:58.320
This might be kind
of a broad question,

01:01:58.320 --> 01:02:03.110
but what would your advice be
to a younger artist who aspires

01:02:03.110 --> 01:02:05.550
to your level of greatness?

01:02:05.550 --> 01:02:08.538
[LAUGHTER]

01:02:13.520 --> 01:02:24.330
I think, well, so to my
participant in the school,

01:02:24.330 --> 01:02:28.665
I always said it's a
matter of life and death.

01:02:28.665 --> 01:02:30.540
If they, within the
question they are asking,

01:02:30.540 --> 01:02:35.100
can't sense the urgency, maybe
it's not the right question.

01:02:35.100 --> 01:02:37.950
And the urgency can hide
itself in very obscure ways,

01:02:37.950 --> 01:02:44.760
but it's really important to,
let's say, to sort of focus

01:02:44.760 --> 01:02:49.330
on why before you focus on how.

01:02:49.330 --> 01:02:51.330
Because a lot of people
said, oh, how do I this,

01:02:51.330 --> 01:02:52.600
how do I that, and so on.

01:02:52.600 --> 01:02:56.040
But the question why, and it's
a much more difficult question

01:02:56.040 --> 01:02:57.000
to ask.

01:02:57.000 --> 01:02:59.640
You know, is this
really necessary?

01:02:59.640 --> 01:03:01.020
Why is this necessary?

01:03:01.020 --> 01:03:02.520
Especially if you're
an artist where

01:03:02.520 --> 01:03:05.140
you seem to be doing a lot
of not so necessary stuff.

01:03:05.140 --> 01:03:09.600
You have to, still, find a
little thing, thin string.

01:03:09.600 --> 01:03:12.030
Maybe it goes down
to a hand sketch.

01:03:12.030 --> 01:03:15.510
Like the little bit of hand
dancing on a piece of paper.

01:03:15.510 --> 01:03:17.190
Maybe that's enough why.

01:03:17.190 --> 01:03:20.600
I don't mean the rational,
big, justifying why.

01:03:20.600 --> 01:03:23.130
Just the kind of
confidence, oh, my god.

01:03:23.130 --> 01:03:25.440
I thought something,
now it's on the paper.

01:03:25.440 --> 01:03:26.670
It's amazing.

01:03:26.670 --> 01:03:29.670
But you have to have the
confidence in the worthwhile.

01:03:29.670 --> 01:03:31.260
And that does take some stamina.

01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:32.730
As we all know,
it's very difficult

01:03:32.730 --> 01:03:35.610
to make a little sort
of doodle on a napkin.

01:03:35.610 --> 01:03:38.250
I mean, to make one that
actually sort of matters

01:03:38.250 --> 01:03:49.710
is very difficult.

01:03:49.710 --> 01:03:50.590
Thank you very much.

01:03:50.590 --> 01:03:51.450
This was extraordinary.

01:03:51.450 --> 01:03:53.117
I don't know how much
time you have here

01:03:53.117 --> 01:03:54.700
in Boston or the area.

01:03:54.700 --> 01:03:56.730
But if you haven't,
I would hope you'd

01:03:56.730 --> 01:04:00.420
have a chance to visit Artists
for Humanity in South Boston.

01:04:00.420 --> 01:04:03.030
Because what you were describing
is this last project, to me,

01:04:03.030 --> 01:04:06.800
so encompasses what is
going on today at AFH.

01:04:06.800 --> 01:04:07.380
Thank you.

01:04:07.380 --> 01:04:08.040
Yes.

01:04:08.040 --> 01:04:10.090
It's, I could not agree more.

01:04:10.090 --> 01:04:11.970
I'm not, I've been
coming to Boston.

01:04:11.970 --> 01:04:13.530
Actually, my first
one person show--

01:04:13.530 --> 01:04:15.720
I should have said
that right away--

01:04:15.720 --> 01:04:19.050
in America, my first one
person show in America

01:04:19.050 --> 01:04:21.180
was at the ICA in Boston.

01:04:21.180 --> 01:04:21.954
And--

01:04:21.954 --> 01:04:23.730
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:23.730 --> 01:04:27.350
Yeah, with Jill
and Jessie Morgan.

01:04:27.350 --> 01:04:30.175
And soon after the first retro--

01:04:30.175 --> 01:04:31.800
what do you call it,
it was not exactly

01:04:31.800 --> 01:04:33.750
the survey show of my work--

01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:35.550
was in San Francisco
that traveled.

01:04:35.550 --> 01:04:37.530
And it just, I'll also
say because Madeleine

01:04:37.530 --> 01:04:38.685
who did that show,
you mentioned it

01:04:38.685 --> 01:04:40.143
at the beginning,
is actually here.

01:04:40.143 --> 01:04:41.760
Aren't you, Madeleine?

01:04:41.760 --> 01:04:42.660
Oh, there you are.

01:04:42.660 --> 01:04:43.260
Hi, Madeleine.

01:04:43.260 --> 01:04:44.280
Yes.

01:04:44.280 --> 01:04:44.780
Hello.

01:04:47.670 --> 01:04:51.270
No, so-- but yeah.

01:04:51.270 --> 01:04:57.300
So you're totally right, and no,
I am not here for long enough.

01:04:57.300 --> 01:04:57.810
Let's see.

01:04:57.810 --> 01:05:01.670
How about all the way
in the back there?

01:05:01.670 --> 01:05:03.956
Yes, that, you.

01:05:03.956 --> 01:05:07.030
Stacy, you're going to have
to stay up there, I'm afraid.

01:05:07.030 --> 01:05:07.620
Thank you.

01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:15.380
Was your work in any way
inspired by Christo's work?

01:05:20.480 --> 01:05:23.000
I like the idea that
Christo could create

01:05:23.000 --> 01:05:28.610
this kind of public attention.

01:05:28.610 --> 01:05:32.630
Wrapping the Reichstag
was just around the time

01:05:32.630 --> 01:05:36.680
where I was going
to Berlin a lot.

01:05:36.680 --> 01:05:43.025
Second week into my art school,
I started in October '89.

01:05:45.670 --> 01:05:48.410
End of October, 2 weeks into
art school, the wall in Berlin

01:05:48.410 --> 01:05:50.810
came down, and I drove
down, and so on and so on.

01:05:50.810 --> 01:05:53.180
And then the Christo, the
Wrapping of the Reichstag

01:05:53.180 --> 01:05:54.500
was very important for me.

01:05:54.500 --> 01:05:58.250
I was actually more into
the dematerialization team

01:05:58.250 --> 01:05:59.480
from the West Coast.

01:05:59.480 --> 01:06:03.050
Robert Irwin-- I love Robert
Irwin-- and James Turrell.

01:06:03.050 --> 01:06:07.070
The people who somehow
suggested that psychology,

01:06:07.070 --> 01:06:13.370
contemplative ideas,
dematerializing art

01:06:13.370 --> 01:06:16.230
could be of some importance.

01:06:16.230 --> 01:06:18.770
Later, I became more interested
in the East Coast people

01:06:18.770 --> 01:06:22.160
like [INAUDIBLE] and the kind
of more sort of structurally

01:06:22.160 --> 01:06:25.230
organized people.

01:06:25.230 --> 01:06:26.790
Christo kind of came later on.

01:06:26.790 --> 01:06:28.360
I was more, like,
Gordon Matta-Clark

01:06:28.360 --> 01:06:29.595
or more Robert Morris.

01:06:34.330 --> 01:06:34.830
Great.

01:06:37.620 --> 01:06:38.160
Oh, hi.

01:06:38.160 --> 01:06:40.710
I'm very interested in
the participatory part

01:06:40.710 --> 01:06:43.530
of your work, and how
the spectator becomes

01:06:43.530 --> 01:06:46.350
a participant, for example,
in the Weather Project.

01:06:46.350 --> 01:06:48.210
So I'm wondering,
to what extent do

01:06:48.210 --> 01:06:50.862
you plan for a particular
reaction, and to what extent

01:06:50.862 --> 01:06:51.570
is it a surprise?

01:06:55.510 --> 01:07:05.270
Well, going to art school in
the late '80s, early '90s,

01:07:05.270 --> 01:07:09.910
there was very rewarding for me
as a certain regime of object

01:07:09.910 --> 01:07:12.090
who'd kind of ended.

01:07:12.090 --> 01:07:13.440
The '80s ended.

01:07:13.440 --> 01:07:15.540
There were some
focus back on kind

01:07:15.540 --> 01:07:17.760
of more political
or activistic ideas.

01:07:17.760 --> 01:07:21.900
But a few amazing artists like
Felix Gonzalez-Torres or Cady

01:07:21.900 --> 01:07:23.410
Noland came out of America.

01:07:23.410 --> 01:07:26.040
Like unbelievably
interesting times.

01:07:26.040 --> 01:07:29.880
And for me, it was a moment
where the avant garde

01:07:29.880 --> 01:07:33.360
idea of freedom, which
was so important for art,

01:07:33.360 --> 01:07:37.410
like that created
modernism, the idea

01:07:37.410 --> 01:07:42.180
was somehow suddenly engaging
again with responsibility.

01:07:42.180 --> 01:07:44.130
Then we had phenomenology
in the '90s.

01:07:44.130 --> 01:07:46.590
We had Deleuze and
people who are thinking

01:07:46.590 --> 01:07:49.680
about the sharing, the
co-producing, the co-authoring,

01:07:49.680 --> 01:07:52.890
the negotiations, the fact that
reality was, to some extent,

01:07:52.890 --> 01:07:53.900
relative.

01:07:53.900 --> 01:07:56.340
Bruno Latour came along with
introduced soft sciences

01:07:56.340 --> 01:07:58.590
or social sciences, and so.

01:07:58.590 --> 01:08:00.638
And I was really
just stepping stones

01:08:00.638 --> 01:08:01.680
through all those people.

01:08:01.680 --> 01:08:03.180
I sound like, I
sound as though--

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:06.930
I don't, as an artist, you can
just hop through all of that

01:08:06.930 --> 01:08:07.710
and enjoy.

01:08:07.710 --> 01:08:09.660
Sort of just breathe
the best parts.

01:08:09.660 --> 01:08:14.227
But the point here is that
combining this idea of freedom

01:08:14.227 --> 01:08:16.060
and the responsibility
that comes with that.

01:08:16.060 --> 01:08:18.018
And then the idea of
responsibility, very much,

01:08:18.018 --> 01:08:20.340
is about-- freedom is
always about me, me, me, me.

01:08:20.340 --> 01:08:21.970
I want to be free.

01:08:21.970 --> 01:08:24.240
And there's a degree
of, at some point,

01:08:24.240 --> 01:08:27.180
you can be so avant garde
that you kind of disconnect.

01:08:27.180 --> 01:08:30.060
You become so much
about your own,

01:08:30.060 --> 01:08:35.490
the conditions of your own,
the freedom of speech, almost.

01:08:35.490 --> 01:08:39.240
So I think my generation, and
I'm lucky to be a part of that

01:08:39.240 --> 01:08:41.729
generation, this sense of
responsibility introduced

01:08:41.729 --> 01:08:45.390
the notion, well you cannot be
responsible if you're only one

01:08:45.390 --> 01:08:46.290
person.

01:08:46.290 --> 01:08:47.040
Like with freedom.

01:08:47.040 --> 01:08:50.069
You have to be at least two
people, and better even more.

01:08:50.069 --> 01:08:53.279
This is where the door is
sort open to the kind of idea

01:08:53.279 --> 01:08:56.191
of the participatory
productivity,

01:08:56.191 --> 01:08:57.149
I would almost call it.

01:08:57.149 --> 01:09:02.279
Considering the visitor to
the museum being almost as

01:09:02.279 --> 01:09:03.750
important as the artist.

01:09:03.750 --> 01:09:05.970
Being an artist, I would
just sort of, just keep it.

01:09:05.970 --> 01:09:08.783
But, and of course,
and the truth is museum

01:09:08.783 --> 01:09:10.450
thinks that they are
the most important.

01:09:10.450 --> 01:09:12.930
So they kind of miss,
they miss the whole thing.

01:09:12.930 --> 01:09:13.627
But the point--

01:09:13.627 --> 01:09:14.901
[LAUGHTER]

01:09:14.901 --> 01:09:16.859
But by all means, if
there would be no artists,

01:09:16.859 --> 01:09:17.942
there would be no museums.

01:09:17.942 --> 01:09:20.700
Let's just sort of make that.

01:09:20.700 --> 01:09:23.245
But then you have the visitors.

01:09:23.245 --> 01:09:25.245
Probably, also, there
would be without visitors,

01:09:25.245 --> 01:09:29.819
there would no-- so I think
the participatory also

01:09:29.819 --> 01:09:34.890
have to do with showing
that trust in people being

01:09:34.890 --> 01:09:39.029
able to use abstraction
for something valuable

01:09:39.029 --> 01:09:40.290
is so important.

01:09:40.290 --> 01:09:41.910
And it just doesn't happen.

01:09:41.910 --> 01:09:47.660
If you look at the ways
that, how should I say?

01:09:47.660 --> 01:09:49.300
Everybody said, look
at the internet.

01:09:49.300 --> 01:09:53.460
Now I had this amazing meeting
with all these so talented

01:09:53.460 --> 01:09:54.720
people up at the Media Lab.

01:09:54.720 --> 01:09:55.447
Several groups.

01:09:55.447 --> 01:09:57.780
And so I said now, I'm very
uncertain whether what I say

01:09:57.780 --> 01:09:59.010
is now actually true.

01:09:59.010 --> 01:10:02.160
But, you know, the tendency
is, of course, people,

01:10:02.160 --> 01:10:03.510
they kind of just consume.

01:10:03.510 --> 01:10:05.430
They don't produce.

01:10:05.430 --> 01:10:07.750
And I've learned today this
is maybe not totally true.

01:10:07.750 --> 01:10:10.800
But generally speaking, I'm
very interested in handing over

01:10:10.800 --> 01:10:13.260
the trust to the people
that they feel that they

01:10:13.260 --> 01:10:16.510
can actually produce realities.

01:10:16.510 --> 01:10:18.140
So I just like a
reality machine.

01:10:18.140 --> 01:10:22.785
But if you don't participate
in it, it doesn't do anything.

01:10:25.410 --> 01:10:27.930
Right there, if you could
just pass the microphone.

01:10:27.930 --> 01:10:28.430
Thank you.

01:10:32.250 --> 01:10:33.000
Hi.

01:10:33.000 --> 01:10:37.620
As a light artist, do you
feel a sense of responsibility

01:10:37.620 --> 01:10:41.160
in promoting solar power
and energy efficiency

01:10:41.160 --> 01:10:42.720
in your installations?

01:10:42.720 --> 01:10:48.450
Like as a sort of role model for
people and artists to follow?

01:10:48.450 --> 01:10:52.260
Or do you choose
that in your work?

01:10:52.260 --> 01:10:54.390
Yeah, to some extent.

01:10:54.390 --> 01:11:00.010
I have, yeah, I'm
not, maybe, the kind

01:11:00.010 --> 01:11:03.770
of typical ecologically
very correct artist.

01:11:03.770 --> 01:11:05.380
I'm also not the worst.

01:11:05.380 --> 01:11:11.200
You know, I think, to be
honest, I have to a great extent

01:11:11.200 --> 01:11:15.070
become involved with
being, like you suggest,

01:11:15.070 --> 01:11:18.400
that, well, I better,
I better start

01:11:18.400 --> 01:11:21.580
using LED bulbs in a sculpture.

01:11:21.580 --> 01:11:25.700
Is somehow, somehow
it's also important.

01:11:25.700 --> 01:11:28.780
But I also have to
say that looking at,

01:11:28.780 --> 01:11:30.750
what do you call it?

01:11:30.750 --> 01:11:32.530
Condensendant lamp is
not the right word.

01:11:32.530 --> 01:11:33.280
[AUDIENCE MURMURS]

01:11:33.280 --> 01:11:34.300
(MUMBLING) Incandescent.

01:11:34.300 --> 01:11:37.450
So then if you
look at that, it's

01:11:37.450 --> 01:11:40.250
just so much more beautiful
than a bloody LED.

01:11:40.250 --> 01:11:46.150
So what do you do if you
want to address beauty?

01:11:46.150 --> 01:11:48.790
But then again, these
lovely LED companies,

01:11:48.790 --> 01:11:51.100
they keep sending me
small samples and say--

01:11:51.100 --> 01:11:53.770
and the quality of the light
actually does get better.

01:11:53.770 --> 01:11:55.270
A lot better, actually.

01:11:55.270 --> 01:11:58.630
And now, you know, we
have great LED panels

01:11:58.630 --> 01:12:02.500
who are very, very interesting.

01:12:02.500 --> 01:12:04.420
But it's important to
understand it's just,

01:12:04.420 --> 01:12:07.090
it's maybe in an
evolution, it is not

01:12:07.090 --> 01:12:08.620
replacing something else.

01:12:08.620 --> 01:12:10.720
It's maybe something
which is just ending,

01:12:10.720 --> 01:12:14.620
and then something
else is taking over.

01:12:14.620 --> 01:12:15.790
So in that sense, yes.

01:12:15.790 --> 01:12:17.830
I have started becoming
much more aware of it.

01:12:17.830 --> 01:12:21.240
It took me some time.

01:12:21.240 --> 01:12:22.360
It did take me some time.

01:12:22.360 --> 01:12:23.705
This is an LED.

01:12:23.705 --> 01:12:24.580
And it's interesting.

01:12:24.580 --> 01:12:27.520
Not a long time ago, that
was an expensive LED.

01:12:27.520 --> 01:12:31.820
Now it's like, this
is a quality LED.

01:12:31.820 --> 01:12:34.600
And now it's, how
much is it, Frederick?

01:12:34.600 --> 01:12:38.410
0.06 cents.

01:12:38.410 --> 01:12:40.600
0.06 cents.

01:12:40.600 --> 01:12:41.860
And it's a good LED.

01:12:41.860 --> 01:12:44.560
It's from-- now every
time I say this--

01:12:44.560 --> 01:12:45.100
I won't say.

01:12:45.100 --> 01:12:47.500
But it's-- we bought it,
so I don't have to say it.

01:12:47.500 --> 01:12:50.440
But this is the price of an LED.

01:12:50.440 --> 01:12:52.840
I remember in seventh--

01:12:52.840 --> 01:12:56.590
no, I started school, '70s.

01:12:56.590 --> 01:12:58.960
There was this amazing
girl in the seventh grade.

01:12:58.960 --> 01:13:01.630
I was like, I was first grade.

01:13:01.630 --> 01:13:03.770
And she had this,
I thought I should

01:13:03.770 --> 01:13:05.020
have said it at the beginning.

01:13:05.020 --> 01:13:07.980
She had this incredible,
so I was very curious.

01:13:07.980 --> 01:13:10.930
She had this amazing
calculator in her bag.

01:13:10.930 --> 01:13:13.060
And I was not exactly
in love with it,

01:13:13.060 --> 01:13:14.470
but I thought she was amazing.

01:13:14.470 --> 01:13:18.490
And then I looked at it, and
then it had all these other,

01:13:18.490 --> 01:13:22.310
it was brown and gold
and they had red digits.

01:13:22.310 --> 01:13:24.800
I just looked at that red color.

01:13:24.800 --> 01:13:27.374
And it's so funny because
it was a Texas Instrument.

01:13:30.370 --> 01:13:34.012
And I was so excited to see
there was the numbers 1 to 9

01:13:34.012 --> 01:13:35.470
and then plus minus,
and then there

01:13:35.470 --> 01:13:38.380
was all the other things
one could press on.

01:13:38.380 --> 01:13:41.560
I was like, by god.

01:13:41.560 --> 01:13:45.580
And the red color of that light.

01:13:45.580 --> 01:13:47.590
So I was, at the
beginning, I remember

01:13:47.590 --> 01:13:50.080
from very early on being
taken about the quality

01:13:50.080 --> 01:13:54.310
of an experience of light
and this incredible girl.

01:13:54.310 --> 01:13:56.770
And, you know, the
engineering effort.

01:13:56.770 --> 01:13:59.890
And it's actually the
first time I came across,

01:13:59.890 --> 01:14:03.190
and I remember very clearly
the Texas Instrument.

01:14:03.190 --> 01:14:07.600
Do you remember they were
kind of gold and brown?

01:14:07.600 --> 01:14:10.660
It's kind of funny.

01:14:10.660 --> 01:14:12.188
Right here.

01:14:12.188 --> 01:14:13.116
Thank you.

01:14:16.370 --> 01:14:17.150
All right.

01:14:17.150 --> 01:14:19.940
How collaborative
is your studio?

01:14:19.940 --> 01:14:20.600
Say that again?

01:14:20.600 --> 01:14:24.840
How collaborative
is your studio?

01:14:24.840 --> 01:14:31.450
It's, I rely a lot on
the talent at the studio.

01:14:31.450 --> 01:14:33.270
There's a lot of things that--

01:14:33.270 --> 01:14:34.920
I've done it all.

01:14:34.920 --> 01:14:36.780
And then at some
point in the process,

01:14:36.780 --> 01:14:38.298
I say, oh, too much logistics.

01:14:38.298 --> 01:14:39.840
I want to only do
the creative stuff.

01:14:39.840 --> 01:14:41.520
Then you ask somebody
to do it, then it

01:14:41.520 --> 01:14:43.590
turns out they do it
much better than--

01:14:43.590 --> 01:14:46.320
or a little bit
better-- than I did.

01:14:46.320 --> 01:14:54.180
And it just so turns
out that, actually, it's

01:14:54.180 --> 01:14:58.140
become a much better run place
after other people assisted

01:14:58.140 --> 01:14:58.900
and took over.

01:14:58.900 --> 01:14:59.800
And so on.

01:14:59.800 --> 01:15:01.740
And now, of course,
where's the [INAUDIBLE]??

01:15:01.740 --> 01:15:04.620
I would like to think that
I'm the artist and the one who

01:15:04.620 --> 01:15:05.610
makes the call.

01:15:05.610 --> 01:15:09.060
And this means that I start
an idea, I start a process.

01:15:09.060 --> 01:15:11.940
Very often it's fed by
an already existing,

01:15:11.940 --> 01:15:13.230
but I start something.

01:15:13.230 --> 01:15:15.570
Then we go, we branch
into five options,

01:15:15.570 --> 01:15:18.240
and we work on five
options, digitally models,

01:15:18.240 --> 01:15:20.790
build it, make it testing,
testing with water,

01:15:20.790 --> 01:15:22.375
underwater with
light or something.

01:15:22.375 --> 01:15:24.750
And then say, oh, the three
options, they are impossible.

01:15:24.750 --> 01:15:25.360
I don't like them.

01:15:25.360 --> 01:15:26.860
Why don't we take
these two options?

01:15:26.860 --> 01:15:28.890
Then everybody works
on these two options,

01:15:28.890 --> 01:15:31.410
and then we build a model
of the space one to one.

01:15:31.410 --> 01:15:34.230
We take the space from them,
literally moves it to Berlin,

01:15:34.230 --> 01:15:35.220
then we build it up.

01:15:35.220 --> 01:15:38.250
And then there's
two teams, almost.

01:15:38.250 --> 01:15:41.280
And then I'm involved.

01:15:41.280 --> 01:15:42.840
Then we bring in theory.

01:15:42.840 --> 01:15:44.830
Criticizing, did somebody
do something similar?

01:15:44.830 --> 01:15:45.830
What were they thinking?

01:15:45.830 --> 01:15:48.360
So 200 years ago, there
was kind of a symphony

01:15:48.360 --> 01:15:49.993
about that particular problem.

01:15:49.993 --> 01:15:51.660
Should we listen to
that while we do it?

01:15:51.660 --> 01:15:53.713
So then a lot of ideas
are coming together.

01:15:53.713 --> 01:15:55.630
So it's a little bit
difficult to say where is

01:15:55.630 --> 01:15:58.020
the exact sort of boundaries.

01:15:58.020 --> 01:16:03.750
But we are, I hope we all
agree that I'm the one who

01:16:03.750 --> 01:16:06.555
makes those sort of final call.

01:16:06.555 --> 01:16:10.340
[LAUGHTER]

01:16:10.340 --> 01:16:12.680
But I have on the other
side, I've always, always

01:16:12.680 --> 01:16:13.850
had an open door policy.

01:16:13.850 --> 01:16:15.710
Because there is
a lot of artists--

01:16:15.710 --> 01:16:19.580
and bless them for that--
who have closed and mystified

01:16:19.580 --> 01:16:20.750
the artist studio.

01:16:20.750 --> 01:16:24.110
And kept, in order to
prevent-- and I would argue,

01:16:24.110 --> 01:16:25.310
for market reasons--

01:16:25.310 --> 01:16:30.860
the kind of, this idea
of sort of keeping secret

01:16:30.860 --> 01:16:33.440
that there is like a painter
who only makes painting and has,

01:16:33.440 --> 01:16:35.570
like, 90 assistants painting.

01:16:35.570 --> 01:16:38.730
[INAUDIBLE] kind of like, sort
of obvious he has to keep it

01:16:38.730 --> 01:16:39.230
closed.

01:16:39.230 --> 01:16:41.570
Because it would sort of
not so good for his market,

01:16:41.570 --> 01:16:42.260
you could say.

01:16:42.260 --> 01:16:43.962
I don't think of
that being a problem.

01:16:43.962 --> 01:16:45.920
I think, why don't
everybody just come and look

01:16:45.920 --> 01:16:46.950
for themselves?

01:16:46.950 --> 01:16:48.410
And I don't mean that literally.

01:16:48.410 --> 01:16:51.230
But the principle
is, that's why I've

01:16:51.230 --> 01:16:52.850
done a number of
works addressing

01:16:52.850 --> 01:16:55.880
the studio as laboratory,
as a sort of producer.

01:16:55.880 --> 01:16:58.460
I worked with the idea
of mixing the museum,

01:16:58.460 --> 01:16:59.810
the studio, the residency.

01:16:59.810 --> 01:17:02.060
The school was in
my studio exactly

01:17:02.060 --> 01:17:04.940
to kind of show the students,
well, let's just be pragmatic.

01:17:04.940 --> 01:17:10.700
Let's be MIT-ish about it, and
sort of think of it as a lab.

01:17:10.700 --> 01:17:13.500
Obviously one would think that
there is a degree of leadership

01:17:13.500 --> 01:17:16.490
at MIT as well, sort
of pushing the things

01:17:16.490 --> 01:17:17.790
in the right direction.

01:17:17.790 --> 01:17:19.380
And I think of my
studio like that.

01:17:19.380 --> 01:17:21.740
And I have no problem
suggesting that art

01:17:21.740 --> 01:17:27.500
can, to a great extent, be a
collaborative effort like that.

01:17:27.500 --> 01:17:29.090
Please, yes.

01:17:29.090 --> 01:17:30.840
Can you talk a little
bit about the nature

01:17:30.840 --> 01:17:32.910
of viewers' engagement
with your work

01:17:32.910 --> 01:17:34.860
after the project is completed?

01:17:34.860 --> 01:17:37.390
Like the Weather Project looks
so fantastic, but none of us

01:17:37.390 --> 01:17:38.490
will ever see it.

01:17:38.490 --> 01:17:40.370
And so there's a
real dilemma there

01:17:40.370 --> 01:17:43.560
where on-site, it
seems very immersive

01:17:43.560 --> 01:17:46.740
and emotional and interactive,
but then we only experience it

01:17:46.740 --> 01:17:50.472
10 years later as a
kind of discourse.

01:17:50.472 --> 01:17:51.680
Yeah, that's a good question.

01:17:58.440 --> 01:18:00.720
So for me, it doesn't
really ever end.

01:18:00.720 --> 01:18:05.910
And it's not like the work
before was somehow less

01:18:05.910 --> 01:18:08.190
important than the actual work.

01:18:08.190 --> 01:18:11.502
And now with the Weather
Project, it did end,

01:18:11.502 --> 01:18:12.210
and it did close.

01:18:15.180 --> 01:18:18.210
I remember, they
wanted to prolong it.

01:18:18.210 --> 01:18:19.415
We did one time prolong it.

01:18:19.415 --> 01:18:21.040
Then they were so
successful they said,

01:18:21.040 --> 01:18:22.830
why don't we run it
for another six weeks?

01:18:22.830 --> 01:18:24.850
And then I said,
no, let's close it.

01:18:24.850 --> 01:18:25.980
It's like so successful.

01:18:25.980 --> 01:18:29.220
Why not close it now?

01:18:29.220 --> 01:18:32.250
But it did continue.

01:18:32.250 --> 01:18:36.780
It turned out, I
was with a professor

01:18:36.780 --> 01:18:38.580
today at the Media Lab.

01:18:38.580 --> 01:18:40.501
What was his name, Lillah?

01:18:40.501 --> 01:18:41.001
Which one?

01:18:41.001 --> 01:18:41.501
I'm sorry.

01:18:41.501 --> 01:18:44.530
The guy who had the photo of
the Weather Project on his book?

01:18:44.530 --> 01:18:45.030
Oh!

01:18:45.030 --> 01:18:46.000
William Uricchio.

01:18:46.000 --> 01:18:46.500
What?

01:18:46.500 --> 01:18:47.850
William Uricchio from CMS.

01:18:47.850 --> 01:18:49.680
William Uricchio CMS.

01:18:49.680 --> 01:18:51.090
So he had a book with--

01:18:51.090 --> 01:18:52.400
so it's sort of still around.

01:18:52.400 --> 01:18:53.400
Do you know what I mean?

01:18:53.400 --> 01:18:55.110
The Weather Project was
on the cover of his book.

01:18:55.110 --> 01:18:56.040
And he still had--

01:18:56.040 --> 01:18:58.120
so funny-- he actually
had to ask for permission,

01:18:58.120 --> 01:18:59.460
but it's an old book.

01:18:59.460 --> 01:19:02.880
And in a way, these
projects, I look at them

01:19:02.880 --> 01:19:04.860
more as kind of
cause and effect.

01:19:04.860 --> 01:19:09.510
So you do something that
sets some motion into action.

01:19:09.510 --> 01:19:12.670
And eventually, something dies
out and something still moves.

01:19:12.670 --> 01:19:16.170
So the project isn't
really over in that sense.

01:19:16.170 --> 01:19:20.040
It was not about the actual
shape of the project.

01:19:20.040 --> 01:19:24.270
It was about the, well,
I shouldn't say science.

01:19:24.270 --> 01:19:27.900
But it was about the kind of
material that it negotiated.

01:19:27.900 --> 01:19:29.820
And that material
is still around.

01:19:29.820 --> 01:19:33.780
The work is still
producing reality.

01:19:33.780 --> 01:19:35.670
In this case, it's
a sort of dialogue.

01:19:35.670 --> 01:19:38.160
It's hopefully being
considered as a work that

01:19:38.160 --> 01:19:41.410
is worth bringing up in
different discussions.

01:19:43.990 --> 01:19:45.752
I have-- yeah.

01:19:45.752 --> 01:19:47.460
Maybe this gentleman
there in the center.

01:19:47.460 --> 01:19:48.460
He's had his hand up.

01:19:48.460 --> 01:19:50.950
If you could just--

01:19:50.950 --> 01:19:51.690
thank you.

01:19:55.450 --> 01:19:59.050
I saw the Weather Project
in London and was impressed.

01:19:59.050 --> 01:20:02.050
But I may be the
only other person

01:20:02.050 --> 01:20:06.520
here with solar, so here's
some light from Cambridge

01:20:06.520 --> 01:20:07.370
from yesterday.

01:20:07.370 --> 01:20:07.870
Great.

01:20:07.870 --> 01:20:11.640
[LAUGHTER]

01:20:11.640 --> 01:20:12.240
Great.

01:20:12.240 --> 01:20:12.740
Great.

01:20:15.830 --> 01:20:19.340
So that's about 10 years
old off the shelf equipment

01:20:19.340 --> 01:20:23.750
that I've been using for my bike
lights every day since then.

01:20:23.750 --> 01:20:26.450
And it's amazing to me that
I'm the only person that I

01:20:26.450 --> 01:20:28.640
see walking around MIT
with something like that,

01:20:28.640 --> 01:20:30.710
or Cambridge, or Boston.

01:20:30.710 --> 01:20:33.350
The very few people
who take care of that.

01:20:33.350 --> 01:20:37.680
In the Little Sun one of the
things that I find about solar

01:20:37.680 --> 01:20:40.410
is that it's currency.

01:20:40.410 --> 01:20:43.380
And local production, in
terms of Gandhian economics.

01:20:43.380 --> 01:20:45.990
Swadeshi local production.

01:20:45.990 --> 01:20:49.470
But light is one currency.

01:20:49.470 --> 01:20:52.650
Electricity, which is
what runs the light,

01:20:52.650 --> 01:20:54.840
also can run AA
batteries, which are

01:20:54.840 --> 01:20:59.010
the most used batteries in the
world, and charge cell phones.

01:20:59.010 --> 01:21:00.930
And there's seven
billion cell phones

01:21:00.930 --> 01:21:03.670
now in the world for seven
and a half billion people.

01:21:03.670 --> 01:21:07.320
And I'm wondering if you're
extending your idea from light

01:21:07.320 --> 01:21:10.770
to the currency of
electricity and the economics

01:21:10.770 --> 01:21:13.380
of the solar Swadeshi.

01:21:13.380 --> 01:21:15.840
Yes, it's a good question.

01:21:15.840 --> 01:21:18.930
And the thing is, I've been
stepping through a departments

01:21:18.930 --> 01:21:21.150
here, and I've been
meeting definitely

01:21:21.150 --> 01:21:23.040
20 people who are
working with solar,

01:21:23.040 --> 01:21:26.050
and small solar objects in the
micro solar systems and so on.

01:21:26.050 --> 01:21:30.210
So if you ask me, there's,
like, solar everywhere.

01:21:30.210 --> 01:21:33.980
But, yes, I know Frederick tells
me not to be talking about it.

01:21:33.980 --> 01:21:35.730
But we are, this summer,
actually bringing

01:21:35.730 --> 01:21:38.640
out the Little Sun
mobile charger.

01:21:38.640 --> 01:21:40.950
Sort of a very
beautiful, exactly

01:21:40.950 --> 01:21:42.270
because of the same thing.

01:21:42.270 --> 01:21:45.240
We are working very hard on
our retail and business model

01:21:45.240 --> 01:21:47.850
and delivery systems and
the whole economic sort

01:21:47.850 --> 01:21:50.160
of so-called BOP economic.

01:21:50.160 --> 01:21:56.040
And we had [INAUDIBLE] input,
also, on some ideas on that.

01:21:56.040 --> 01:21:58.470
So I didn't say that before.

01:21:58.470 --> 01:22:00.720
You should really not hesitate
to go to littlesun.com.

01:22:00.720 --> 01:22:03.143
And there's a few introductions.

01:22:03.143 --> 01:22:04.560
Some are more
technical, if you're

01:22:04.560 --> 01:22:06.270
more in the kind of
nerdy area, and some

01:22:06.270 --> 01:22:09.180
are more emotional if you're
more sort of creative.

01:22:09.180 --> 01:22:12.630
But what I'm actually asking
you is not quite going to do it.

01:22:12.630 --> 01:22:14.850
You have to, then,
there's all that like it,

01:22:14.850 --> 01:22:18.450
send it into Instagram, and
all that social media stuff,

01:22:18.450 --> 01:22:19.710
which we live off.

01:22:19.710 --> 01:22:22.530
So this is how people actually
learn about this story

01:22:22.530 --> 01:22:23.460
and bring about.

01:22:23.460 --> 01:22:27.600
And we have brought, and I
didn't bring that number.

01:22:27.600 --> 01:22:31.530
We have produced 250,000 lamps.

01:22:31.530 --> 01:22:36.390
One, about 180 we have
already delivered.

01:22:36.390 --> 01:22:39.990
30%, one third, has been
delivered in off grid.

01:22:39.990 --> 01:22:42.630
So it's a great equation.

01:22:42.630 --> 01:22:44.940
This means we have in the
two-- one and a half year,

01:22:44.940 --> 01:22:45.960
almost two years--

01:22:45.960 --> 01:22:48.570
we have actually
had a robust curve.

01:22:48.570 --> 01:22:50.850
This is why I have to
tell you, you need to,

01:22:50.850 --> 01:22:52.517
it's not enough for
me to talk about it.

01:22:52.517 --> 01:22:54.620
You need to go and then
say, send it a friend,

01:22:54.620 --> 01:22:56.460
oh, let's send it to
a friend and like it

01:22:56.460 --> 01:22:58.840
and Facebook and all of that.

01:22:58.840 --> 01:23:01.260
Simply, this is what
has been driving us.

01:23:01.260 --> 01:23:03.990
So the online communities,
the sharing the stories,

01:23:03.990 --> 01:23:06.600
the uploading
images, has been one

01:23:06.600 --> 01:23:08.857
of the drivers in the
success, which we also

01:23:08.857 --> 01:23:11.190
talked about here at MIT,
because there's so much talent

01:23:11.190 --> 01:23:12.478
in that field as well.

01:23:12.478 --> 01:23:13.520
So it's very interesting.

01:23:13.520 --> 01:23:15.300
It's not just
engineering and art.

01:23:15.300 --> 01:23:18.420
It's also communication, retail
policy making, new economies,

01:23:18.420 --> 01:23:20.760
economical model,
micro distribution,

01:23:20.760 --> 01:23:24.690
last mile successes, and so on.

01:23:24.690 --> 01:23:27.690
I have rarely, it's like
being a young artist again.

01:23:27.690 --> 01:23:31.570
I have learned so much
from this project.

01:23:31.570 --> 01:23:33.520
I have also learned
to be humble.

01:23:33.520 --> 01:23:36.338
That's very new for me.

01:23:36.338 --> 01:23:38.933
[LAUGHTER]

01:23:38.933 --> 01:23:40.350
I think we have
time for one more.

01:23:40.350 --> 01:23:41.130
Yes, please.

01:23:43.860 --> 01:23:46.080
I mean this in the
broadest sense possible.

01:23:46.080 --> 01:23:48.300
But to you, what is
the place of spectacle

01:23:48.300 --> 01:23:51.470
when you're making art?

01:23:51.470 --> 01:23:54.680
I'm sorry, say that again?

01:23:54.680 --> 01:23:56.900
For you, what is the
place of spectacle

01:23:56.900 --> 01:24:00.960
when you're making art
or thinking about art?

01:24:00.960 --> 01:24:05.970
So I work a lot with the sort
of experiential potential.

01:24:05.970 --> 01:24:09.260
And of course, I would want
people to take a groundbreaking

01:24:09.260 --> 01:24:11.450
experience, and that
could be a sort of micro,

01:24:11.450 --> 01:24:14.270
or should I say nano
groundbreaking experience.

01:24:14.270 --> 01:24:16.820
Or a really groundbreaking,
the big sort

01:24:16.820 --> 01:24:20.390
of pathetic groundbreaking
type of experience.

01:24:20.390 --> 01:24:22.610
But I'm also quite
aware of, there

01:24:22.610 --> 01:24:26.480
is economy, which is very
active within the notion

01:24:26.480 --> 01:24:27.620
of the spectacle.

01:24:27.620 --> 01:24:30.960
And that economy has brought
about the experience industry,

01:24:30.960 --> 01:24:32.540
or experience economy.

01:24:32.540 --> 01:24:36.560
And of course, the kind
of shallow or hollowness

01:24:36.560 --> 01:24:40.970
of the spectacle with
regards to facilitating

01:24:40.970 --> 01:24:46.140
a profitable idea of how to
drive the experience economy.

01:24:46.140 --> 01:24:48.530
So I'm sometimes,
frankly speaking,

01:24:48.530 --> 01:24:54.500
torn between how can I
suggest that what I do

01:24:54.500 --> 01:24:56.390
is not just hollow?

01:24:56.390 --> 01:25:00.530
But how do you, in the world
today, it's also hard to see.

01:25:00.530 --> 01:25:02.692
And look at something
and go, like, well,

01:25:02.692 --> 01:25:04.400
does this person
actually really mean it?

01:25:04.400 --> 01:25:06.830
Or is it just some
kind of la la la?

01:25:06.830 --> 01:25:09.990
And it's difficult, and
it's getting difficult,

01:25:09.990 --> 01:25:12.560
especially as the museums
and the institutions that

01:25:12.560 --> 01:25:15.440
facilitate, they are
forced in our society

01:25:15.440 --> 01:25:18.260
into systems where
they need to quantify

01:25:18.260 --> 01:25:20.840
their successes by virtue of
telling the politicians so

01:25:20.840 --> 01:25:23.780
on so many numbers, so on
so much success criteria

01:25:23.780 --> 01:25:26.600
that you can actually measure
is how we run the museum.

01:25:26.600 --> 01:25:29.990
And of course, then the
artists, you go like, huh?

01:25:29.990 --> 01:25:33.980
What about if my
show was not sort

01:25:33.980 --> 01:25:37.910
of a high hitter in numbers?

01:25:37.910 --> 01:25:40.160
Does that mean it
wasn't good art?

01:25:40.160 --> 01:25:42.050
So of course, we are
seeing situations

01:25:42.050 --> 01:25:44.420
where the spectacle
is kind of becoming

01:25:44.420 --> 01:25:48.530
an ambiguous situation, where
a museum is kind of forced

01:25:48.530 --> 01:25:50.270
into a situation,
where if we are going

01:25:50.270 --> 01:25:53.030
to keep up the funding, we have
to deliver something like that.

01:25:53.030 --> 01:25:54.230
And then they call me.

01:25:54.230 --> 01:25:54.740
Right?

01:25:54.740 --> 01:25:55.790
And then I have to--

01:25:55.790 --> 01:25:57.260
[LAUGHTER]

01:25:57.260 --> 01:26:00.740
--then I have to
have that bit of sort

01:26:00.740 --> 01:26:06.050
of a kind of dancing with
the museum about, well,

01:26:06.050 --> 01:26:09.810
instead of talking about how,
we should talk about why.

01:26:09.810 --> 01:26:11.600
And that takes a
little time, sometimes.

01:26:11.600 --> 01:26:14.420
Because once you're into
the discussion of why,

01:26:14.420 --> 01:26:17.690
normally you can nail the
museum down to the values

01:26:17.690 --> 01:26:19.660
that they used
when they started.

01:26:19.660 --> 01:26:21.660
And this is, I think,
where it gets interesting.

01:26:21.660 --> 01:26:24.620
And then you can bring museums
into the sort of risk zone.

01:26:24.620 --> 01:26:26.552
And then you obviously
need to not just call

01:26:26.552 --> 01:26:28.760
the fire department and say,
oh, it's going to be OK.

01:26:28.760 --> 01:26:30.980
Just let's do it.

01:26:30.980 --> 01:26:31.898
It's going to be OK.

01:26:31.898 --> 01:26:33.440
You also call the
politician and say,

01:26:33.440 --> 01:26:35.060
why don't we take that risk?

01:26:35.060 --> 01:26:37.640
By god, for god's sake,
it's about art, anyway.

01:26:37.640 --> 01:26:39.590
It's not about
politicians and so on.

01:26:39.590 --> 01:26:43.400
And sadly, this is getting
increasingly difficult to say.

01:26:43.400 --> 01:26:47.750
Museums being so forced into
the traditional commerce,

01:26:47.750 --> 01:26:49.970
the commodification
of our sense,

01:26:49.970 --> 01:26:51.850
of the experience
economy, blah blah blah.

01:26:51.850 --> 01:26:54.450
So there is a very
important area there.

01:26:54.450 --> 01:26:56.450
And we also have
to say in America,

01:26:56.450 --> 01:26:59.190
the private sector is the one
that is supporting the museums.

01:26:59.190 --> 01:27:02.210
We need the private sector
to be very risk oriented.

01:27:02.210 --> 01:27:03.680
And as we know,
the private sector

01:27:03.680 --> 01:27:06.118
is not necessarily the
most risk oriented.

01:27:06.118 --> 01:27:07.910
[INAUDIBLE] by all
means, the public sector

01:27:07.910 --> 01:27:10.290
is not risk oriented as well.

01:27:10.290 --> 01:27:12.050
So it's a very
interesting discussion.

01:27:12.050 --> 01:27:15.770
How do we facilitate a space
in which an experience can

01:27:15.770 --> 01:27:20.240
be about the experience and
not about all that other stuff?

01:27:20.240 --> 01:27:21.110
Thank you so much.

01:27:21.110 --> 01:27:22.075
Thank you.

01:27:22.075 --> 01:27:25.470
[APPLAUSE]

01:27:30.805 --> 01:27:32.260
Thank you.

01:27:32.260 --> 01:27:33.230
[INAUDIBLE]

01:27:36.140 --> 01:27:37.250
Just a word.

01:27:37.250 --> 01:27:40.700
The Council for the Arts at
MIT has demonstrated, again,

01:27:40.700 --> 01:27:43.580
impeccable taste in
selecting Olafur.

01:27:43.580 --> 01:27:47.090
And I've got to say, if
Frederick Ottesen is very MIT,

01:27:47.090 --> 01:27:48.670
so are you.

01:27:48.670 --> 01:27:49.400
Yeah.

01:27:49.400 --> 01:27:50.390
Delighted you're here.

01:27:50.390 --> 01:27:50.960
Thank you.

01:27:50.960 --> 01:27:51.890
Thank you, everybody.

01:27:51.890 --> 01:27:53.990
[APPLAUSE]

01:27:55.790 --> 01:27:57.640
Thank you.

